{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b840883c1e7b4010", "type": "tool_result", "content": "200\t \n201\t \n202\t\n203\tarXiv:2608.10360v1 [cs.HC] 11 Aug 2026\n204\t\n205\tMazzikaAI: A knowledge-based performance-to-prompt compiler for\n206\treal-time Arabic maqam accompaniment with a streaming text-to-music model\n207\t\n208\tJiaxin Du\n209\t\n210\tdujia@gvsu.edu\n211\t\n212\tBoulbaba Abdeljaouad\n213\t\n214\tboulbeba.abdeljaouad@gmail.com\n215\t\n216\tYong Zhuang\n217\t\n218\tzhuangyo@gvsu.edu\n219\t\n220\tHaoyu Li\n221\t\n222\tlihao@gvsu.edu\n223\t\n224\tAbstract\n225\t\n226\tArabic maqam music—microtonal, modal, and built on ornamented call-and-response—is among the traditions most underserved by generative music models, whose training frameworks remain predominantly Western and equal-tempered. Real-time accompaniment sharpens this gap: an AI partner must listen, adapt dynamically, and respect idiomatic microtonal structures. Streaming text-to-music models provide strong generative capabilities but lack precise control interfaces. We present MazzikaAI, a knowledge-based system that uses natural language as the actuator of a real-time control loop. By compiling live MIDI, gesture, and inferred harmony into continuously updated text prompts, MazzikaAI steers an unmodified streaming generator (Google Lyria RealTime) without requiring model fine-tuning. The system embeds expert knowledge of six core maqamat, characteristic ornaments, and ensemble dynamics, maintaining real-time responsiveness with sub-second key-to-audible-update latency. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that dynamic prompt compilation reliably grounds generation in microtonal scales, significantly increasing off-grid quarter-tone content over baseline generation. Beyond its core implementation, MazzikaAI illustrates how deterministic knowledge-based rules can effectively bridge expert, non-Western musical traditions and un-fine-tuned foundation models. This architecture establishes a scalable paradigm for real-time human-AI co-creation, offering a generalizable blueprint for interactive accompaniment, adaptive music education, and culturally inclusive generative audio across diverse global idioms.\n227\t\n228\tkeywords: \n229\tExpert system , Knowledge-based system , Prompt engineering , Real-time music generation , Human–AI co-creation , Arabic maqam\n230\t\n231\t††journal: Expert Systems with Applications\n232\t\n233\t\\affiliation\n234\t\n235\t[gvsu]organization=Grand Valley State University,\n236\tcity=Allendale,\n237\tstate=MI,\n238\tcountry=USA\n239\t\n240\t1 Introduction\n241\t\n242\tA good accompanist listens continuously, anticipates, and supports the soloist\n243\twithout overtaking them. Reproducing that behaviour computationally has\n244\tmotivated decades of work on interactive music systems, from rule- and\n245\tagent-based improvisers (Lewis, 2000; Rowe, 1993) to\n246\tstatistical style companions (Pachet, 2003; Thom, 2000). Neural\n247\tmusic models have transformed the raw quality of what can be generated:\n248\ttext-conditioned systems synthesize convincing full-band audio across a wide\n249\tstylistic range (Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023; Evans et al., 2024).\n250\tThe property that makes them powerful—end-to-end generation of audio from a\n251\tcoarse conditioning signal—also makes them hard to steer at the\n252\ttemporal grain a live performance demands. A model that renders a beautiful\n253\tminute of music from one prompt is not, by itself, a bandmate that reacts to\n254\tthe note just played.\n255\t\n256\tStreaming, prompt-steerable models such as Lyria RealTime (Google DeepMind, 2025) and\n257\tMagenta RealTime (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) narrow the gap: they emit audio\n258\tincrementally and accept prompt updates mid-session. But they supply a\n259\tcontrol surface without a control policy. They answer “given\n260\tthis prompt now, what audio streams out?” and leave open the question a live\n261\taccompaniment system must answer: how should that prompt be produced and\n262\tcontinuously revised from what a human is playing at this instant? Driven by a\n263\tstatic prompt, a streaming model produces a generic backing track that ignores\n264\tthe soloist. Symbolic and score-conditioned\n265\tmodels (Huang et al., 2019; Roberts et al., 2018; Hadjeres et al., 2017)\n266\toffer fine control but forgo the timbral realism of audio models, and adapting\n267\teither family to a new idiom normally means collecting data and fine-tuning.\n268\tThe problem is sharper outside the Western tonal mainstream: Arabic maqam\n269\tmusic, built on microtonal intervals, characteristic ornaments, and modal\n270\tresting tones, is poorly represented in both the training distributions and the\n271\tcontrol vocabularies of mainstream\n272\tmodels (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021; Bozkurt et al., 2014).\n273\t\n274\tApproach\n275\t\n276\tOur premise is that natural language is a\n277\tmodel-agnostic control surface for this class of models, and that it can be\n278\tdriven as a control law rather than authored as a query. If a system can\n279\tcontinuously translate what a musician is doing—register, dynamics, the\n280\tphrase just ended, the mode, the chord being held—into precise,\n281\tmusically-literate prose, a general streaming model becomes a responsive band.\n282\tThis reframes interactive accompaniment as compilation: build a compiler\n283\tfrom live performance state to text prompts and let an off-the-shelf generator\n284\tdo the synthesis. The intelligence lives in the translation layer—in knowing\n285\tthat a half-flat second degree is the expressive soul of maqam bayati, that an\n286\tanswering phrase should echo the soloist’s last pitches, and that naming only\n287\tthe active instruments prevents the model from defaulting to a full band.\n288\t\n289\tViewed from the expert-systems side, the resulting design has a familiar shape\n290\tand one unfamiliar component. MazzikaAI is organized as a classical\n291\tknowledge-based system (Ebcioğlu, 1988; Rowe, 1993): an\n292\texplicit, hand-authored knowledge base; a working memory holding\n293\tthe performance state estimated from the live event stream; and a\n294\tdeterministic, rule-based inference layer. What is new is the effector.\n295\tWhere a classical expert system renders its conclusions through a symbolic\n296\tsynthesizer, MazzikaAI renders them through a large pretrained generator\n297\taddressed in prose—a neuro-symbolic division of labour in which inspectable\n298\tsymbolic expertise supplies idiom and moment-to-moment intent while the\n299\tfoundation model supplies raw musical competence.\n300\t\n301\tMazzikaAI realizes this as a deployed system (Fig. 1). A human\n302\tplays a MIDI keyboard in the browser; a React front end captures note events,\n303\tsustain, hand gestures, and voice commands and streams them over a WebSocket to\n304\ta Python backend. The backend maintains a rolling estimate of the\n305\tperformance—density, register, motion, dynamic arc, phrase boundaries,\n306\tinferred harmony—and, on each meaningful change, compiles that state into a\n307\tprompt streamed to a text-to-music model (i.e. Lyria RealTime). Two generative streams run concurrently: a\n308\tmelodic ensemble that follows and answers the soloist, and a\n309\ttempo-locked percussion bed. Returned audio is decoded and scheduled for\n310\tlow-latency playback beneath the player’s own piano. The system is designed\n311\taround vintage Arabic maqam performance—its deepest knowledge, bespoke\n312\tcontroller, and all output-quality experiments target that idiom—and drives\n313\tthe model entirely through prompt design; the same compiler retargets to\n314\teight further Western genres by authoring new prompt fragments alone\n315\t(Appendix A).\n316\t\n317\tContributions.\n318\t\n319\t1. \n320\t\n321\tPrompt compilation as a real-time control law. We formulate\n322\tcontinuous natural-language prompt synthesis as the actuator of a\n323\tknowledge-based controller (§4): a deterministic\n324\tcompiler C:(st,qt)↦ptC:(s_{t},q_{t})\\mapsto p_{t} over an estimated performance state,\n325\ta four-state call-and-response policy, and a coarse\n326\tcontrol-signature gate that decides when re-steering the live\n327\tstream is musically warranted. This turns an unmodified streaming\n328\ttext-to-music model into a responsive accompanist with no fine-tuning,\n329\tand is independent of the particular generator behind it.\n330\t\n331\t2. \n332\t\n333\tA maqam-centred musical knowledge base with prompt-level\n334\tmechanisms that substitute for absent API control. We contribute an\n335\tinspectable, hand-authored knowledge base (Table 4) whose\n336\tcore is the Arabic tradition: six maqamat with explicit quarter-tone\n337\tspellings, characteristic degrees, ornaments, resting tones and\n338\tnegative guidance, and a takht ensemble with three role registers per\n339\tinstrument—extended by nine further Western modes and eight further\n340\tgenre configurations that demonstrate the adaptation cost\n341\t(Appendix A). Two mechanisms attempt control the model\n342\tAPI does not expose:\n343\tan ordered instrument-constraint clause intended to stand in\n344\tfor stem-level mixing, and explicit maqam grounding that steers a\n345\tWestern-centric generator toward microtonal modal behaviour without\n346\ttraining data. Our ablations quantify the effect of each\n347\t(§6.4).\n348\t\n349\t3. \n350\t\n351\tAn instrumented evaluation with controlled ablations and\n352\texpert playing sessions. From end-to-end instrumentation of live\n353\tsessions we report a full latency decomposition\n354\t(Figs. 3–4) and gating and\n355\tstreaming-stability measurements (Fig. 5). A replay\n356\tharness then drives input-identical sessions through ablated\n357\tvariants (§6.4), measuring maqam grounding\n358\t(significantly more quarter-tone melodic content), gating (API\n359\teconomy, with stability robust even to ungated re-steering), and the\n360\tcompiler’s contribution (a static prompt collapses responsiveness).\n361\tPlaying sessions with two expert musicians provide a perceptual\n362\tevaluation that localizes the principal remaining gap at beat-level\n363\tentrainment (§6.3).\n364\t\n365\t§2 positions the system;\n366\t§3 gives the architecture; §4 the\n367\tcompiler; §5 the implementation;\n368\t§6 the measurements, ablation experiments, and\n369\tperceptual evaluation; §7 discussion and limitations;\n370\tAppendix A catalogues the genre configurations.\n371\t\n372\t2 Related Work and Positioning\n373\t\n374\tTable 1 places MazzikaAI against the five families of prior\n375\twork it draws on, along the axes that matter for live accompaniment: what the\n376\tsystem is steered through, at what granularity, what it costs to\n377\tadapt to a new idiom, whether it generates streaming audio, and\n378\twhether it supports microtonal non-Western modes.\n379\t\n380\tTable 1: Positioning of MazzikaAI against related families. “Granularity” is the finest interval at which the system’s behavior can be redirected; “adaptation cost” is what a new idiom requires.\n381\t\n382\t \n383\t\n384\tFamily\n385\t\n386\t \n387\t\n388\tControl\n389\t\n390\tsurface\n391\t\n392\t \n393\t\n394\tGranularity\n395\t\n396\t \n397\t\n398\tAdaptation\n399\t\n400\tcost\n401\t\n402\t \n403\t\n404\tStreaming\n405\t\n406\taudio\n407\t\n408\t \n409\t\n410\tMicrotonal\n411\t\n412\tKnowledge-based symbolic1\n413\t\n414\thand-authored rules\n415\t\n416\tnote/voice\n417\t\n418\tnew rules\n419\t\n420\tno\n421\tpossible\n422\t\n423\tInteractive symbolic improvisers2\n424\t\n425\tlistening ++ bespoke composer\n426\t\n427\tnote/beat\n428\t\n429\tredesign or retrain\n430\t\n431\tno\n432\trare\n433\t\n434\tSymbolic neural3\n435\t\n436\tlatents / partial scores\n437\t\n438\tnote\n439\t\n440\tfine-tune\n441\t\n442\tno\n443\tno\n444\t\n445\tText-conditioned audio4\n446\t\n447\tone-shot text prompt\n448\t\n449\twhole clip\n450\t\n451\tfine-tune / prompt\n452\t\n453\tno\n454\tno\n455\t\n456\tStreaming audio models5\n457\t\n458\tlive text ++ scalars\n459\t\n460\tchunk (∼\\sim2 s)\n461\t\n462\tmanual prompting\n463\t\n464\tyes\n465\tuntested\n466\t\n467\tMazzikaAI\n468\t\n469\tcompiled text, closed loop\n470\t\n471\tchange-gated chunk\n472\t\n473\tnew prompt fragment\n474\t\n475\tyes\n476\tgrounded\n477\t\n478\t1(Ebcioğlu, 1988; Cope, 1992); 2(Rowe, 1993; Lewis, 2000; Pachet, 2003; Thom, 2000; Scarlatos et al., 2025); 3(Huang et al., 2019; Roberts et al., 2018; Hadjeres et al., 2017); 4(Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023; Evans et al., 2024); 5(Google DeepMind, 2025; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025).\n479\t\n480\tKnowledge-based systems for music\n481\t\n482\tEncoding musical expertise as an\n483\texplicit rule base is among the oldest ambitions of intelligent systems.\n484\tEbcioğlu’s CHORAL (Ebcioğlu, 1988) harmonized Bach chorales with\n485\thundreds of hand-authored rules; Cope’s EMI (Cope, 1992) recombined\n486\tcorpus signatures under rule-based control; Rowe (Rowe, 1993)\n487\torganized machine musicianship as listening, interpretation, and rule-governed\n488\tresponse. MazzikaAI deliberately retains the two properties these share—the\n489\tknowledge is inspectable and the inference is deterministic—and\n490\treplaces only what historically bounded them, the symbolic effector, with a\n491\tpretrained streaming generator addressed in prose, in the spirit of recent\n492\tefforts to unify foundation models with explicit knowledge\n493\trepresentations (Pan et al., 2024).\n494\t\n495\tInteractive and co-creative systems\n496\t\n497\tVoyager (Lewis, 2000)\n498\tnegotiated musical agency alongside a human; the Continuator (Pachet, 2003)\n499\tlearned a player’s style online and extended their phrases;\n500\tBoB (Thom, 2000) traded solos with a musician; and\n501\tReaLJam (Scarlatos et al., 2025) recently demonstrated live human–AI\n502\tjamming with a reinforcement-learning-tuned transformer. The turn-taking\n503\tdynamics these systems exploit are well documented\n504\tethnomusicologically (Monson, 1996). MazzikaAI performs the same\n505\tlisten-then-respond cycle, but couples the listening machinery to a large\n506\tpretrained audio model mediated by language rather than to a bespoke\n507\tsymbolic composer.\n508\t\n509\tNeural music generation and streaming\n510\t\n511\tSymbolic models predict note\n512\tevents; raw-audio and latent-audio models—WaveNet (van den Oord et al., 2016),\n513\tJukebox (Dhariwal et al., 2020), AudioLM (Borsos et al., 2023), and\n514\tthe text-conditioned MusicLM (Agostinelli et al., 2023),\n515\tMusicGen (Copet et al., 2023), Stable Audio (Evans et al., 2024),\n516\tRiffusion (Forsgren and Martiros, 2022), built over neural codecs such as\n517\tEnCodec (Défossez et al., 2023)—model timbre and production directly but\n518\tare designed for offline, one-shot synthesis. Streaming\n519\tmodels (Google DeepMind, 2025; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) are the closest substrate; MazzikaAI\n520\tis built on Lyria RealTime. The Live Music Models\n521\treport (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) describes two adjacent capabilities: audio\n522\tinjection, which conditions the model on raw input audio rather than on an\n523\tinterpreted model of the performance, and manual prompt steering by a human\n524\toperator. Closing that loop automatically and symbolically—estimating a\n525\tstructured performance state and compiling it, through an explicit knowledge\n526\tbase, into a continuously refreshed prompt and generation config—is the gap\n527\tMazzikaAI fills.\n528\t\n529\tPrompting as control\n530\t\n531\tPrompting emerged in NLP as a way to steer\n532\tfrozen models (Brown et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2023), but almost always as a\n533\tone-shot, text-in–text-out operation. MazzikaAI extends it along two\n534\taxes at once: from one-shot authoring to a continuous closed-loop regime\n535\tin which the prompt string is the actuator of a real-time feedback law, and\n536\tfrom text output to streaming audio. To our knowledge, treating prompt\n537\tengineering as the control law of an interactive audio system is a framing\n538\tprior prompting work does not develop.\n539\t\n540\tNon-Western modal music\n541\t\n542\tArabic maqam and Turkish makam are\n543\torganized around modal scales with microtonal degrees, signature\n544\tornaments, and resting tones. Computational work has focused largely on\n545\tanalysis—the review of computational makam\n546\tresearch (Bozkurt et al., 2014) documents the tuning and intonation problems that\n547\tdefeat twelve-tone tools, and recent work classifies maqamat from\n548\taudio (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021)—while generation of authentic microtonal\n549\tmodal music remains underexplored. MazzikaAI addresses this asymmetry by\n550\tgrounding rather than retraining: prompts spell each maqam out\n551\texplicitly, naming the microtonal degrees, ornaments and resting tones and\n552\tsupplying negative guidance against Western tonality\n553\t(§4.4).\n554\t\n555\t3 System Architecture\n556\t\n557\tMazzikaAI is a three-tier system—browser client, Python backend, cloud\n558\tgenerative model—connected by a single bidirectional WebSocket\n559\t(Fig. 1). Four design commitments shape it. (i) Text is the\n560\tsole point of control: the generator is a black box that consumes prose and\n561\temits audio, so the deployed model can be replaced without touching the control\n562\tlayer. (ii) Idiom is injected, not learned: every prompt carries explicit\n563\tgrounding for the active mode, including negative guidance against Western\n564\ttonality. (iii) The performer conducts: rather than a fixed band, the\n565\tplayer toggles ensemble sections in and out and the compiler actively suppresses\n566\tthe rest. (iv) Re-steering is gated: a coarse control signature decides\n567\twhen the live stream may be re-prompted, trading responsiveness against\n568\tstability.\n569\t\n570\tBrowser client\n571\t\n572\tFastAPI backend\n573\t\n574\tCloud models\n575\tMIDIGestureVoiceSheetWeb SocketWeb Audio out (dual contexts, mix)PlayerSession(working memory)Controllerqt,θtq_{t},\\theta_{t}Promptcompiler CCLyriaEngine(melodic)LyriaEngine(percussion)Gemini OMRLyria RealTimeJSONpromptpromptaudio_chunk\n576\t\n577\tFigure 1: End-to-end architecture. Performer input is serialized as JSON\n578\tWebSocket messages to a FastAPI backend, which updates the working memory,\n579\tselects an accompaniment state qtq_{t} and generation parameters θt\\theta_{t}, and\n580\tcompiles a prompt ptp_{t}. Two independent Lyria RealTime sessions—a following\n581\tmelodic engine and an independent tempo-locked percussion engine—return\n582\t4848 kHz stereo PCM that is returned as audio_chunk messages and\n583\tmixed client-side beneath the performer’s local piano.\n584\t\n585\tClient and transport\n586\t\n587\tA React/TypeScript application captures four\n588\tinput modalities: note events (note_on/note_off, velocity,\n589\tCC64 sustain) through the Web MIDI API (W3C, 2015); hand gestures\n590\trecognized on-device with MediaPipe Hands (Lugaresi et al., 2019); spoken\n591\tcommands through the Web Speech API; and a Learn Mode that parses MusicXML\n592\tin-browser and delegates scanned PDF pages to Gemini optical music recognition.\n593\tThe performer’s own notes are rendered locally by a sampled grand piano, so the\n594\thuman’s sound is never delayed by the network round trip. Every input becomes a\n595\tsmall JSON message: midi_event, control_update (genre,\n596\tmaqam, tempo; debounced to ∼400{\\sim}400 ms), gesture,\n597\tsection_toggle, and a tick heartbeat every 250250 ms that\n598\tadvances time-dependent state when no notes arrive. The backend replies with\n599\tstate_update messages (estimated state and compiled prompt) and\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "810faa67-2c27-451c-a35b-94f5fe6397c3", "uuid": "30926efb-589d-44a2-b7e1-43517525064f", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T06:33:49.594Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mazzika_text.txt", "content": " \n \n\narXiv:2608.10360v1 [cs.HC] 11 Aug 2026\n\nMazzikaAI: A knowledge-based performance-to-prompt compiler for\nreal-time Arabic maqam accompaniment with a streaming text-to-music model\n\nJiaxin Du\n\ndujia@gvsu.edu\n\nBoulbaba Abdeljaouad\n\nboulbeba.abdeljaouad@gmail.com\n\nYong Zhuang\n\nzhuangyo@gvsu.edu\n\nHaoyu Li\n\nlihao@gvsu.edu\n\nAbstract\n\nArabic maqam music—microtonal, modal, and built on ornamented call-and-response—is among the traditions most underserved by generative music models, whose training frameworks remain predominantly Western and equal-tempered. Real-time accompaniment sharpens this gap: an AI partner must listen, adapt dynamically, and respect idiomatic microtonal structures. Streaming text-to-music models provide strong generative capabilities but lack precise control interfaces. We present MazzikaAI, a knowledge-based system that uses natural language as the actuator of a real-time control loop. By compiling live MIDI, gesture, and inferred harmony into continuously updated text prompts, MazzikaAI steers an unmodified streaming generator (Google Lyria RealTime) without requiring model fine-tuning. The system embeds expert knowledge of six core maqamat, characteristic ornaments, and ensemble dynamics, maintaining real-time responsiveness with sub-second key-to-audible-update latency. Empirical evaluations demonstrate that dynamic prompt compilation reliably grounds generation in microtonal scales, significantly increasing off-grid quarter-tone content over baseline generation. Beyond its core implementation, MazzikaAI illustrates how deterministic knowledge-based rules can effectively bridge expert, non-Western musical traditions and un-fine-tuned foundation models. This architecture establishes a scalable paradigm for real-time human-AI co-creation, offering a generalizable blueprint for interactive accompaniment, adaptive music education, and culturally inclusive generative audio across diverse global idioms.\n\nkeywords: \nExpert system , Knowledge-based system , Prompt engineering , Real-time music generation , Human–AI co-creation , Arabic maqam\n\n††journal: Expert Systems with Applications\n\n\\affiliation\n\n[gvsu]organization=Grand Valley State University,\ncity=Allendale,\nstate=MI,\ncountry=USA\n\n1 Introduction\n\nA good accompanist listens continuously, anticipates, and supports the soloist\nwithout overtaking them. Reproducing that behaviour computationally has\nmotivated decades of work on interactive music systems, from rule- and\nagent-based improvisers (Lewis, 2000; Rowe, 1993) to\nstatistical style companions (Pachet, 2003; Thom, 2000). Neural\nmusic models have transformed the raw quality of what can be generated:\ntext-conditioned systems synthesize convincing full-band audio across a wide\nstylistic range (Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023; Evans et al., 2024).\nThe property that makes them powerful—end-to-end generation of audio from a\ncoarse conditioning signal—also makes them hard to steer at the\ntemporal grain a live performance demands. A model that renders a beautiful\nminute of music from one prompt is not, by itself, a bandmate that reacts to\nthe note just played.\n\nStreaming, prompt-steerable models such as Lyria RealTime (Google DeepMind, 2025) and\nMagenta RealTime (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) narrow the gap: they emit audio\nincrementally and accept prompt updates mid-session. But they supply a\ncontrol surface without a control policy. They answer “given\nthis prompt now, what audio streams out?” and leave open the question a live\naccompaniment system must answer: how should that prompt be produced and\ncontinuously revised from what a human is playing at this instant? Driven by a\nstatic prompt, a streaming model produces a generic backing track that ignores\nthe soloist. Symbolic and score-conditioned\nmodels (Huang et al., 2019; Roberts et al., 2018; Hadjeres et al., 2017)\noffer fine control but forgo the timbral realism of audio models, and adapting\neither family to a new idiom normally means collecting data and fine-tuning.\nThe problem is sharper outside the Western tonal mainstream: Arabic maqam\nmusic, built on microtonal intervals, characteristic ornaments, and modal\nresting tones, is poorly represented in both the training distributions and the\ncontrol vocabularies of mainstream\nmodels (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021; Bozkurt et al., 2014).\n\nApproach\n\nOur premise is that natural language is a\nmodel-agnostic control surface for this class of models, and that it can be\ndriven as a control law rather than authored as a query. If a system can\ncontinuously translate what a musician is doing—register, dynamics, the\nphrase just ended, the mode, the chord being held—into precise,\nmusically-literate prose, a general streaming model becomes a responsive band.\nThis reframes interactive accompaniment as compilation: build a compiler\nfrom live performance state to text prompts and let an off-the-shelf generator\ndo the synthesis. The intelligence lives in the translation layer—in knowing\nthat a half-flat second degree is the expressive soul of maqam bayati, that an\nanswering phrase should echo the soloist’s last pitches, and that naming only\nthe active instruments prevents the model from defaulting to a full band.\n\nViewed from the expert-systems side, the resulting design has a familiar shape\nand one unfamiliar component. MazzikaAI is organized as a classical\nknowledge-based system (Ebcioğlu, 1988; Rowe, 1993): an\nexplicit, hand-authored knowledge base; a working memory holding\nthe performance state estimated from the live event stream; and a\ndeterministic, rule-based inference layer. What is new is the effector.\nWhere a classical expert system renders its conclusions through a symbolic\nsynthesizer, MazzikaAI renders them through a large pretrained generator\naddressed in prose—a neuro-symbolic division of labour in which inspectable\nsymbolic expertise supplies idiom and moment-to-moment intent while the\nfoundation model supplies raw musical competence.\n\nMazzikaAI realizes this as a deployed system (Fig. 1). A human\nplays a MIDI keyboard in the browser; a React front end captures note events,\nsustain, hand gestures, and voice commands and streams them over a WebSocket to\na Python backend. The backend maintains a rolling estimate of the\nperformance—density, register, motion, dynamic arc, phrase boundaries,\ninferred harmony—and, on each meaningful change, compiles that state into a\nprompt streamed to a text-to-music model (i.e. Lyria RealTime). Two generative streams run concurrently: a\nmelodic ensemble that follows and answers the soloist, and a\ntempo-locked percussion bed. Returned audio is decoded and scheduled for\nlow-latency playback beneath the player’s own piano. The system is designed\naround vintage Arabic maqam performance—its deepest knowledge, bespoke\ncontroller, and all output-quality experiments target that idiom—and drives\nthe model entirely through prompt design; the same compiler retargets to\neight further Western genres by authoring new prompt fragments alone\n(Appendix A).\n\nContributions.\n\n1. \n\nPrompt compilation as a real-time control law. We formulate\ncontinuous natural-language prompt synthesis as the actuator of a\nknowledge-based controller (§4): a deterministic\ncompiler C:(st,qt)↦ptC:(s_{t},q_{t})\\mapsto p_{t} over an estimated performance state,\na four-state call-and-response policy, and a coarse\ncontrol-signature gate that decides when re-steering the live\nstream is musically warranted. This turns an unmodified streaming\ntext-to-music model into a responsive accompanist with no fine-tuning,\nand is independent of the particular generator behind it.\n\n2. \n\nA maqam-centred musical knowledge base with prompt-level\nmechanisms that substitute for absent API control. We contribute an\ninspectable, hand-authored knowledge base (Table 4) whose\ncore is the Arabic tradition: six maqamat with explicit quarter-tone\nspellings, characteristic degrees, ornaments, resting tones and\nnegative guidance, and a takht ensemble with three role registers per\ninstrument—extended by nine further Western modes and eight further\ngenre configurations that demonstrate the adaptation cost\n(Appendix A). Two mechanisms attempt control the model\nAPI does not expose:\nan ordered instrument-constraint clause intended to stand in\nfor stem-level mixing, and explicit maqam grounding that steers a\nWestern-centric generator toward microtonal modal behaviour without\ntraining data. Our ablations quantify the effect of each\n(§6.4).\n\n3. \n\nAn instrumented evaluation with controlled ablations and\nexpert playing sessions. From end-to-end instrumentation of live\nsessions we report a full latency decomposition\n(Figs. 3–4) and gating and\nstreaming-stability measurements (Fig. 5). A replay\nharness then drives input-identical sessions through ablated\nvariants (§6.4), measuring maqam grounding\n(significantly more quarter-tone melodic content), gating (API\neconomy, with stability robust even to ungated re-steering), and the\ncompiler’s contribution (a static prompt collapses responsiveness).\nPlaying sessions with two expert musicians provide a perceptual\nevaluation that localizes the principal remaining gap at beat-level\nentrainment (§6.3).\n\n§2 positions the system;\n§3 gives the architecture; §4 the\ncompiler; §5 the implementation;\n§6 the measurements, ablation experiments, and\nperceptual evaluation; §7 discussion and limitations;\nAppendix A catalogues the genre configurations.\n\n2 Related Work and Positioning\n\nTable 1 places MazzikaAI against the five families of prior\nwork it draws on, along the axes that matter for live accompaniment: what the\nsystem is steered through, at what granularity, what it costs to\nadapt to a new idiom, whether it generates streaming audio, and\nwhether it supports microtonal non-Western modes.\n\nTable 1: Positioning of MazzikaAI against related families. “Granularity” is the finest interval at which the system’s behavior can be redirected; “adaptation cost” is what a new idiom requires.\n\n \n\nFamily\n\n \n\nControl\n\nsurface\n\n \n\nGranularity\n\n \n\nAdaptation\n\ncost\n\n \n\nStreaming\n\naudio\n\n \n\nMicrotonal\n\nKnowledge-based symbolic1\n\nhand-authored rules\n\nnote/voice\n\nnew rules\n\nno\npossible\n\nInteractive symbolic improvisers2\n\nlistening ++ bespoke composer\n\nnote/beat\n\nredesign or retrain\n\nno\nrare\n\nSymbolic neural3\n\nlatents / partial scores\n\nnote\n\nfine-tune\n\nno\nno\n\nText-conditioned audio4\n\none-shot text prompt\n\nwhole clip\n\nfine-tune / prompt\n\nno\nno\n\nStreaming audio models5\n\nlive text ++ scalars\n\nchunk (∼\\sim2 s)\n\nmanual prompting\n\nyes\nuntested\n\nMazzikaAI\n\ncompiled text, closed loop\n\nchange-gated chunk\n\nnew prompt fragment\n\nyes\ngrounded\n\n1(Ebcioğlu, 1988; Cope, 1992); 2(Rowe, 1993; Lewis, 2000; Pachet, 2003; Thom, 2000; Scarlatos et al., 2025); 3(Huang et al., 2019; Roberts et al., 2018; Hadjeres et al., 2017); 4(Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023; Evans et al., 2024); 5(Google DeepMind, 2025; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025).\n\nKnowledge-based systems for music\n\nEncoding musical expertise as an\nexplicit rule base is among the oldest ambitions of intelligent systems.\nEbcioğlu’s CHORAL (Ebcioğlu, 1988) harmonized Bach chorales with\nhundreds of hand-authored rules; Cope’s EMI (Cope, 1992) recombined\ncorpus signatures under rule-based control; Rowe (Rowe, 1993)\norganized machine musicianship as listening, interpretation, and rule-governed\nresponse. MazzikaAI deliberately retains the two properties these share—the\nknowledge is inspectable and the inference is deterministic—and\nreplaces only what historically bounded them, the symbolic effector, with a\npretrained streaming generator addressed in prose, in the spirit of recent\nefforts to unify foundation models with explicit knowledge\nrepresentations (Pan et al., 2024).\n\nInteractive and co-creative systems\n\nVoyager (Lewis, 2000)\nnegotiated musical agency alongside a human; the Continuator (Pachet, 2003)\nlearned a player’s style online and extended their phrases;\nBoB (Thom, 2000) traded solos with a musician; and\nReaLJam (Scarlatos et al., 2025) recently demonstrated live human–AI\njamming with a reinforcement-learning-tuned transformer. The turn-taking\ndynamics these systems exploit are well documented\nethnomusicologically (Monson, 1996). MazzikaAI performs the same\nlisten-then-respond cycle, but couples the listening machinery to a large\npretrained audio model mediated by language rather than to a bespoke\nsymbolic composer.\n\nNeural music generation and streaming\n\nSymbolic models predict note\nevents; raw-audio and latent-audio models—WaveNet (van den Oord et al., 2016),\nJukebox (Dhariwal et al., 2020), AudioLM (Borsos et al., 2023), and\nthe text-conditioned MusicLM (Agostinelli et al., 2023),\nMusicGen (Copet et al., 2023), Stable Audio (Evans et al., 2024),\nRiffusion (Forsgren and Martiros, 2022), built over neural codecs such as\nEnCodec (Défossez et al., 2023)—model timbre and production directly but\nare designed for offline, one-shot synthesis. Streaming\nmodels (Google DeepMind, 2025; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) are the closest substrate; MazzikaAI\nis built on Lyria RealTime. The Live Music Models\nreport (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) describes two adjacent capabilities: audio\ninjection, which conditions the model on raw input audio rather than on an\ninterpreted model of the performance, and manual prompt steering by a human\noperator. Closing that loop automatically and symbolically—estimating a\nstructured performance state and compiling it, through an explicit knowledge\nbase, into a continuously refreshed prompt and generation config—is the gap\nMazzikaAI fills.\n\nPrompting as control\n\nPrompting emerged in NLP as a way to steer\nfrozen models (Brown et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2023), but almost always as a\none-shot, text-in–text-out operation. MazzikaAI extends it along two\naxes at once: from one-shot authoring to a continuous closed-loop regime\nin which the prompt string is the actuator of a real-time feedback law, and\nfrom text output to streaming audio. To our knowledge, treating prompt\nengineering as the control law of an interactive audio system is a framing\nprior prompting work does not develop.\n\nNon-Western modal music\n\nArabic maqam and Turkish makam are\norganized around modal scales with microtonal degrees, signature\nornaments, and resting tones. Computational work has focused largely on\nanalysis—the review of computational makam\nresearch (Bozkurt et al., 2014) documents the tuning and intonation problems that\ndefeat twelve-tone tools, and recent work classifies maqamat from\naudio (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021)—while generation of authentic microtonal\nmodal music remains underexplored. MazzikaAI addresses this asymmetry by\ngrounding rather than retraining: prompts spell each maqam out\nexplicitly, naming the microtonal degrees, ornaments and resting tones and\nsupplying negative guidance against Western tonality\n(§4.4).\n\n3 System Architecture\n\nMazzikaAI is a three-tier system—browser client, Python backend, cloud\ngenerative model—connected by a single bidirectional WebSocket\n(Fig. 1). Four design commitments shape it. (i) Text is the\nsole point of control: the generator is a black box that consumes prose and\nemits audio, so the deployed model can be replaced without touching the control\nlayer. (ii) Idiom is injected, not learned: every prompt carries explicit\ngrounding for the active mode, including negative guidance against Western\ntonality. (iii) The performer conducts: rather than a fixed band, the\nplayer toggles ensemble sections in and out and the compiler actively suppresses\nthe rest. (iv) Re-steering is gated: a coarse control signature decides\nwhen the live stream may be re-prompted, trading responsiveness against\nstability.\n\nBrowser client\n\nFastAPI backend\n\nCloud models\nMIDIGestureVoiceSheetWeb SocketWeb Audio out (dual contexts, mix)PlayerSession(working memory)Controllerqt,θtq_{t},\\theta_{t}Promptcompiler CCLyriaEngine(melodic)LyriaEngine(percussion)Gemini OMRLyria RealTimeJSONpromptpromptaudio_chunk\n\nFigure 1: End-to-end architecture. Performer input is serialized as JSON\nWebSocket messages to a FastAPI backend, which updates the working memory,\nselects an accompaniment state qtq_{t} and generation parameters θt\\theta_{t}, and\ncompiles a prompt ptp_{t}. Two independent Lyria RealTime sessions—a following\nmelodic engine and an independent tempo-locked percussion engine—return\n4848 kHz stereo PCM that is returned as audio_chunk messages and\nmixed client-side beneath the performer’s local piano.\n\nClient and transport\n\nA React/TypeScript application captures four\ninput modalities: note events (note_on/note_off, velocity,\nCC64 sustain) through the Web MIDI API (W3C, 2015); hand gestures\nrecognized on-device with MediaPipe Hands (Lugaresi et al., 2019); spoken\ncommands through the Web Speech API; and a Learn Mode that parses MusicXML\nin-browser and delegates scanned PDF pages to Gemini optical music recognition.\nThe performer’s own notes are rendered locally by a sampled grand piano, so the\nhuman’s sound is never delayed by the network round trip. Every input becomes a\nsmall JSON message: midi_event, control_update (genre,\nmaqam, tempo; debounced to ∼400{\\sim}400 ms), gesture,\nsection_toggle, and a tick heartbeat every 250250 ms that\nadvances time-dependent state when no notes arrive. The backend replies with\nstate_update messages (estimated state and compiled prompt) and", "numLines": 400, "startLine": 200, "totalLines": 2437}}}
