{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8788478118b0db70", "type": "tool_result", "content": "600\taudio_chunk messages tagged\n601\tsource∈\\in{melody,percussion}.\n602\t\n603\tBackend and output\n604\t\n605\tA FastAPI server maintains a\n606\tPlayerSession whose working memory is a rolling window of the last\n607\t128128 note events (§4.2). A controller maps that state to an\n608\taccompaniment behaviour and a generation-parameter vector, and the compiler\n609\tsynthesizes the prompt (§4.5). Prompt and parameters are pushed\n610\tto a live Lyria session whose background receive loop pulls 4848 kHz 1616-bit\n611\tstereo PCM (Google DeepMind, 2025; Défossez et al., 2023), WAV-packs and base64-encodes\n612\tit, and enqueues it on a bounded queue (maxsize=8=8, oldest dropped\n613\twhen full) so buffered audio cannot grow without bound. The client decodes each\n614\tchunk with the Web Audio API and schedules playback on two separate\n615\tAudioContexts using a running “next start” cursor with a small\n616\tnegative overlap (−40-40 ms melody, −20-20 ms percussion) intended to crossfade\n617\tchunk boundaries. The two AI streams are attenuated (0.45×0.45\\times melody,\n618\t0.38×0.38\\times percussion) beneath the performer’s local piano, so arbitration\n619\tbetween the voices is a deterministic gain decision rather than a generative\n620\tone.\n621\t\n622\t3.1 The Accompaniment State Machine\n623\t\n624\tThe controller resolves the estimated performance state into one of four\n625\tbehaviours (Fig. 2). Supporting: while the\n626\tsoloist plays, the band comps quietly beneath the line at the highest\n627\tprompt-adherence and lowest density. Responding: when a phrase ends, an\n628\tanswer window opens ∼0.3{\\sim}0.3–2.52.5 s afterward, in which the prompt lists\n629\tthe soloist’s just-played pitches and instructs an expressive echo.\n630\tSustain: a single note held for >2.5{>}2.5 s is read as a held tone and\n631\tthe accompaniment stays minimal. LongIdle: after >5{>}5 s of silence\n632\twith sections active, the band assumes the floor and leads freely at the\n633\thighest density. Two gates bound the machine: a warmup gate requires two\n634\tnotes after a section is conducted in before the ensemble may sound, and a\n635\tsilence stop halts the melodic stream after >4{>}4 s of silence,\n636\tforcing a fresh re-prompt when the player returns.\n637\t\n638\tSUPPORTINGcomp beneathRESPONDINGanswer phraseSUSTAINminimalLONG IDLEband leadsphrase ends(>>0.40.4 s)plays again1 note held>>2.52.5 snew notesilent>>55 splays(≥\\geq22 warmup)warmup gate:≥\\geq22 notesmelodic stream stops after >>44 s silence\n639\tFigure 2: The four-state accompaniment policy π\\pi. Transitions are driven by\n640\tplaying and silence timers over the performance stream; a warmup gate admits\n641\tthe ensemble and a silence stop halts the melodic stream until the soloist\n642\tresumes.\n643\t\n644\t3.2 Why Two Decoupled Streams\n645\t\n646\tMazzikaAI runs two independent Lyria sessions because melodic accompaniment\n647\tand rhythmic bed obey different clocks. The melodic engine has\n648\tmute_drums enabled and no fixed BPM lock; it is re-prompted as\n649\tharmony, register and phrasing evolve. The percussion engine, once\n650\ttriggered by gesture, runs continuously as a tempo-locked 4/44/4 bed at the\n651\tselected BPM, independent of the soloist, cycling through escalating style\n652\tpatterns and using tighter generation parameters to stay literal and sparse.\n653\tTheir control signals genuinely conflict: the melodic voice must be free to\n654\tbreathe, slow, or rest to follow the performer, whereas a groove must remain\n655\tmetronomically steady, and folding both into one prompt would force one to\n656\tcompromise the other. Separation also isolates a hard model constraint—Lyria\n657\tcannot change BPM on a live stream, so a tempo change forces a full\n658\tdisconnect-and-reconnect, which the percussion engine can absorb without\n659\tinterrupting the melodic conversation.\n660\t\n661\t4 Method: Compiling Performance into Prompts\n662\t\n663\t4.1 Problem Formulation\n664\t\n665\tWe treat companion accompaniment as a real-time transduction problem. Let the\n666\tsoloist produce timestamped events e1,e2,…e_{1},e_{2},\\dots, each a MIDI note-on/off with\n667\tvelocity, a sustain-pedal change, a gesture, or a control update. Rather than\n668\tcondition the generator on the raw stream, the system maintains an estimated\n669\tperformance state\n670\t\n671\tst=Φ​({ei:t−W≤τi≤t}),s_{t}=\\Phi\\!\\left(\\{e_{i}:t-W\\leq\\tau_{i}\\leq t\\}\\right),\n672\t\n673\twhere the estimator Φ\\Phi (§4.2) runs over a bounded rolling\n674\twindow—a deque of the last 128128 note events, with feature-specific look-back\n675\thorizons WW of 22–3030 s. A discrete controller π\\pi maps the state to one\n676\tof four accompaniment behaviours,\n677\tqt=π​(st)∈{LongIdle,Responding,Supporting,Sustain}q_{t}=\\pi(s_{t})\\in\\{\\textsc{LongIdle},\\textsc{Responding},\\textsc{Supporting},\\textsc{Sustain}\\}, and Θ\\Theta maps (qt,st)(q_{t},s_{t}) to a genre-conditioned\n678\tgeneration-parameter vector\n679\tθt=(temperature,top-​k,guidance,density,brightness)\\theta_{t}=(\\text{temperature},\\text{top-}k,\\text{guidance},\\text{density},\\text{brightness}) (§4.3).\n680\t\n681\tThe method’s core is a deterministic prompt compiler\n682\t\n683\tC:(st,qt)⟼pt∈Σ∗,C:(s_{t},q_{t})\\;\\longmapsto\\;p_{t}\\in\\Sigma^{*},\n684\t\n685\ta pure, side-effect-free function emitting a single natural-language string over\n686\tthe alphabet Σ\\Sigma of English text (§4.5). Determinism is\n687\tload-bearing: identical states yield identical prompts, which is what makes the\n688\tdownstream gate sound. The generator GG—invoked as a streaming,\n689\tweighted-prompt latent music model (Google DeepMind, 2025)—produces\n690\tat=G​(pt,θt)a_{t}=G(p_{t},\\theta_{t}). Two instances of GG run concurrently\n691\t(§3.2). To avoid destabilizing the live stream, compiler\n692\toutput reaches GG only when a coarse control signature changes:\n693\t\n694\tre-prompt at ​t⇔σ​(st,qt,θt)≠σ​(st−,qt−,θt−).\\text{re-prompt at }t\\iff\\sigma(s_{t},q_{t},\\theta_{t})\\neq\\sigma(s_{t^{-}},q_{t^{-}},\\theta_{t^{-}}).\n695\t\n696\tState estimation, discrete control, deterministic text synthesis, gated\n697\tstreaming: these four stages structure the rest of the section.\n698\t\n699\t4.2 Working Memory: Performance-State Estimation\n700\t\n701\tEstimation is driven by two inputs: MIDI events, which mutate state on arrival,\n702\tand the 250250 ms heartbeat, which recomputes time-dependent features when no\n703\tnote arrives. Table 2 lists the principal features and their\n704\thorizons.\n705\t\n706\tRegister is the mean active pitch (low<50{<}50, high>68{>}68,\n707\telse mid) and texture the number of simultaneously held keys. Over\n708\tshort windows the estimator derives motion (sign of summed pitch differences of\n709\tthe last five onsets), melodic centre and its direction, playing speed from\n710\tnotes-per-second over 33 s, and a dynamic arc by comparing the early and late\n711\thalves of the last twelve velocities. Phrase segmentation is silence-driven:\n712\tinter-onset gaps below 0.350.35 s are legato continuation, gaps above 0.40.4 s\n713\twhile silent mark a phrase end, and gaps above 2.52.5 s mark idle. A phrase end\n714\tmore than 1.21.2 s after the previous one increments a verse counter that\n715\tquantizes into a verse_stage (opening/developing/full), so the accompaniment opens up as the session matures. At each\n716\tcounted phrase end the system captures last_phrase_pitches, the\n717\tunique pitches of the just-ended phrase in order, so the ensemble can echo them\n718\tliterally.\n719\t\n720\tFor harmonically grounded genres the estimator performs two independent forms of\n721\tchord inference. Real-time recognition matches the pitch-class set of\n722\tcurrently held keys against interval templates, trying every pitch class as\n723\tcandidate root and scoring higher-information chords first (dominant/minor\n724\tseventh >> triad >> power chord >> dyad >> octave), yielding a symbol such\n725\tas A7 or Dm. When no chord is held, implied-chord\n726\tinference estimates the current region of the twelve-bar blues form by scoring\n727\tthe recent pitch-class histogram against the three functional harmonies, with\n728\teach tonic weighted ×3\\times 3:\n729\t\n730\tscoreA7 (I)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{A7 (I)}}\n731\t=3​#​(A)+#​(E)+#​(G),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(A)+\\#(E)+\\#(G),\n732\t\n733\tscoreD7 (IV)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{D7 (IV)}}\n734\t=3​#​(D)+#​(A)+#​(C),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(D)+\\#(A)+\\#(C),\n735\t\n736\tscoreE7 (V)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{E7 (V)}}\n737\t=3​#​(E)+#​(B)+#​(D),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(E)+\\#(B)+\\#(D),\n738\t\n739\treturning the arg-max region.\n740\t\n741\tTwo derived features exist purely to stabilize the downstream stream. The\n742\tharmony fingerprint is a coarse pitch-class snapshot over the last\n743\t22 s, quantized into 22-semitone buckets—tuple(sorted(set((p % 12)//2 for p in pitches)))—so that adjacent\n744\tsemitones collapse and the fingerprint is meant to change only when harmony\n745\tgenuinely shifts. It replaced an earlier key that changed on every note-on and\n746\tre-prompted the model continuously during melodic runs; §6.2\n747\treports how far short of its intent it falls. The style profile is a\n748\tnatural-language sentence recomputed every two verses from 3030 s of history\n749\tusing genre-aware vocabulary, e.g. \"ascending blues runs climbing\n750\ttoward the high note, wide bends and leaps, wailing in the high register\".\n751\t\n752\tTable 2: Working memory: principal features of the estimated performance state\n753\tsts_{t}, with the horizon each is computed over.\n754\t\n755\tFeature\n756\t\n757\tHorizon\n758\t\n759\tValues / role\n760\t\n761\tTexture and dynamics\n762\t\n763\tregister\n764\t\n765\theld keys\n766\t\n767\tlow / mid / high (mean pitch)\n768\t\n769\ttexture_hint\n770\t\n771\theld keys\n772\t\n773\tsolo / duo / small / full chord\n774\t\n775\tmotion\n776\t\n777\t5 onsets\n778\t\n779\tascending / descending / static\n780\t\n781\tmelodic_center\n782\t\n783\t6 onsets\n784\t\n785\tmean pitch (register target)\n786\t\n787\tregister_follow\n788\t\n789\t6 onsets\n790\t\n791\trise / fall / hold\n792\t\n793\tplaying_speed\n794\t\n795\t3 s\n796\t\n797\tfast / moderate / slow (notes s-1)\n798\t\n799\tdynamic_arc\n800\t\n801\t12 velocities\n802\t\n803\tcrescendo / diminuendo / steady\n804\t\n805\tPhrase and session time\n806\t\n807\tphrase_state\n808\t\n809\tsilence gap\n810\t\n811\tstart / continue / end / idle\n812\t\n813\tverse_count →\\to stage\n814\t\n815\tsession\n816\t\n817\topening / developing / full\n818\t\n819\tcall_response_active\n820\t\n821\t0.3–2.5 s\n822\t\n823\tpost-phrase answer window\n824\t\n825\tlong_idle\n826\t\n827\t>5{>}5 s\n828\t\n829\tplayer silent; band should lead\n830\t\n831\tis_sustained_hold\n832\t\n833\t>2.5{>}2.5 s\n834\t\n835\tone note held\n836\t\n837\tlast_phrase_pitches\n838\t\n839\tphrase end\n840\t\n841\tpitches to echo back\n842\t\n843\tHarmony\n844\t\n845\tdetected_chord\n846\t\n847\theld keys\n848\t\n849\ttemplate-matched chord symbol\n850\t\n851\timplied_chord\n852\t\n853\t2 s\n854\t\n855\t12-bar region (A7 / D7 / E7)\n856\t\n857\trecent_pitch_context\n858\t\n859\tbuffer\n860\t\n861\tlast 10 unique note names\n862\t\n863\tharmony_fingerprint\n864\t\n865\t2 s\n866\t\n867\tcoarse PC buckets; gate input\n868\t\n869\tstyle_profile\n870\t\n871\t30 s\n872\t\n873\tNL sentence, recomputed / 2 verses\n874\t\n875\t4.3 Controller and Generation Parameters\n876\t\n877\tThe controller selects qtq_{t} by a simple interpretable priority\n878\t(LongIdle >> Responding >> Supporting, with\n879\tSustain as the special case of a single held note): the player actively\n880\tplaying yields Supporting; the open answer window or an ending mode\n881\tyields Responding; sustained silence with sections active yields\n882\tLongIdle.\n883\t\n884\tEach state maps to a numeric control vector read from a per-genre table, which\n885\tstores for each of the nine genres a\n886\t(temperature,top-​k,guidance,density)(\\text{temperature},\\text{top-}k,\\text{guidance},\\text{density}) quadruple per\n887\tstate plus three register-dependent brightness values—135135 hand-tuned values\n888\tin total. Table 3 shows a representative slice. The rationale is\n889\tconsistent across genres: higher temperature is more exploratory; lower\n890\ttop-kk and higher guidance bind the model more tightly to the prompt; density\n891\ttargets note sparsity. LongIdle therefore uses the highest temperature\n892\tand density (the band improvises freely) while Supporting uses the\n893\tlowest temperature and density and the highest guidance (the band stays\n894\tliteral and out of the way). A verse-stage boost of 0.00/0.03/0.060.00/0.03/0.06 is added to\n895\tdensity (capped at 0.900.90) so the accompaniment enriches gradually, and\n896\tbrightness is selected by the soloist’s register so a lead voice cuts through in\n897\tthe high register. The percussion engine uses markedly tighter settings (blues:\n898\ttemperature 0.320.32, top-kk 88, guidance 10.510.5, density 0.320.32) so it plays\n899\tthe pattern rather than improvising fills.\n900\t\n901\tTable 3: Representative generation parameters θt=Θ​(qt,st)\\theta_{t}=\\Theta(q_{t},s_{t}), four\n902\tof nine genres. Density excludes the verse-stage boost; brightness is\n903\tregister-dependent and omitted. The remaining knobs the API\n904\texposes—bpm (fixed at connect) and mute_drums (set for the\n905\tmelodic engine)—are constant per engine.\n906\t\n907\tGenre\n908\tState\n909\ttemp\n910\ttop-kk\n911\tguid.\n912\tdens.\n913\t\n914\tblues\n915\tLongIdle\n916\t0.78\n917\t22\n918\t5.5\n919\t0.58\n920\t\n921\tResponding\n922\t0.72\n923\t18\n924\t6.0\n925\t0.42\n926\t\n927\tSupporting\n928\t0.60\n929\t12\n930\t7.0\n931\t0.22\n932\t\n933\tvintage arabic\n934\tLongIdle\n935\t0.72\n936\t20\n937\t5.8\n938\t0.48\n939\t\n940\tResponding\n941\t0.65\n942\t16\n943\t6.5\n944\t0.38\n945\t\n946\tSupporting\n947\t0.52\n948\t12\n949\t7.5\n950\t0.20\n951\t\n952\tjazz fusion\n953\tLongIdle\n954\t0.82\n955\t24\n956\t5.0\n957\t0.55\n958\t\n959\tResponding\n960\t0.74\n961\t20\n962\t5.8\n963\t0.40\n964\t\n965\tSupporting\n966\t0.64\n967\t14\n968\t6.8\n969\t0.22\n970\t\n971\tclassical\n972\tLongIdle\n973\t0.60\n974\t16\n975\t6.5\n976\t0.45\n977\t\n978\tResponding\n979\t0.55\n980\t14\n981\t7.0\n982\t0.32\n983\t\n984\tSupporting\n985\t0.48\n986\t10\n987\t8.0\n988\t0.18\n989\t\n990\tRe-prompt gating\n991\t\n992\tA live stream cannot tolerate re-prompting on every\n993\tnote. The controller therefore emits a coarse control signature σ\\sigma—a\n994\ttuple of only the musically meaningful determinants of the output: genre, maqam,\n995\tthe LongIdle/Responding/answer-window flags, the harmony\n996\tfingerprint rather than raw notes, the first four echo pitches, the\n997\tactive section set, verse stage, density and brightness rounded into coarse\n998\tbuckets, BPM, and the detected and implied chords. The transport layer pushes a\n999\tnew prompt only when σ\\sigma changes. By design this makes the signature\n1000\tinvariant to the note-by-note churn of a melodic run and sensitive only to\n1001\tgenuine change; §6.2 measures how well that holds.\n1002\t\n1003\t4.4 The Knowledge Base\n1004\t\n1005\tEverything the system knows about music is hand-authored prose and numbers,\n1006\tinventoried in Table 4. Its core is the Arabic tradition: six\n1007\tmaqamat with quarter-tone spellings and a five-instrument takht\n1008\tconfiguration—cello, oud, strings, qanun, and nay (lead)—driven by a\n1009\tbespoke controller with idiom-specific harmonic and echo logic. Around that\n1010\tcore the same structures carry eight further Western genres (1515 mode\n1011\tdescriptions, 3838 ensemble sections with three role registers each, 135135\n1012\tgeneration parameters, and 3636 percussion patterns in total); the full genre\n1013\tcatalogue, which exists chiefly as evidence that retargeting costs only new\n1014\tprompt fragments, is given in Appendix A. The knowledge base\n1015\tis small enough to read in an afternoon.\n1016\t\n1017\tTable 4: Inventory of the hand-authored knowledge base. Every entry is\n1018\thuman-readable text or a tuned constant; none is learned. Line counts are of the\n1019\tdeployed implementation.\n1020\t\n1021\tComponent\n1022\t\n1023\tSize\n1024\t\n1025\tContent and prompt slot\n1026\t\n1027\tMode / scale descriptions\n1028\t\n1029\t15\n1030\t\n1031\t6 Arabic maqamat (bayati, rast, hijaz, nahawand, saba, kurd) with\n1032\tquarter-tone spellings ++ 9 Western scales and modes; each gives note\n1033\tspelling, characteristic degree, ornaments, resting tones, colour, and\n1034\tnegative guidance →\\to maqam line\n1035\t\n1036\tEnsemble sections\n1037\t\n1038\t38\n1039\t\n1040\tacross 9 genres; each with a label and three role registers\n1041\t(supporting, responding, solo_responding),\n1042\ti.e. 114 role descriptions →\\to role assignment\n1043\t\n1044\tGenre configurations\n1045\t\n1046\t9\n1047\t\n1048\tsection order (low→\\tohigh), lead-preference list, header text,\n1049\tno-percussion clause (Appendix A, Table 8)\n1050\t\n1051\tGeneration parameters\n1052\t\n1053\t135\n1054\t\n1055\t99 genres ×\\times (33 states ×\\times 44 knobs ++ 33 brightness\n1056\tvalues) →\\to θt\\theta_{t} (Table 3)\n1057\t\n1058\tPercussion patterns\n1059\t\n1060\t36\n1061\t\n1062\t99 genre families ×\\times 44 escalating styles, each with a tempo anchor\n1063\tand a no-melody exclusion clause\n1064\t\n1065\tHarmonic rules\n1066\t\n1067\t—\n1068\t\n1069\tinterval-template chord recognition, 12-bar region inference, four-tier\n1070\tblues harmonic-lock priority\n1071\t\n1072\tImplementation\n1073\t\n1074\t2,4342{,}434 lines of compiler,\n1075\t246246 of controller, 571571 of state estimation (Python)\n1076\t\n1077\tMaqam grounding\n1078\t\n1079\tTo hold a general model inside a modal world it was\n1080\tnot trained to privilege, the compiler injects a dense scale description. The\n1081\tcanonical entry is Listing 1.\n1082\t\n1083\tListing 1: The bayati grounding clause, verbatim from the\n1084\tknowledge base. All 15 mode entries follow this template: spelling,\n1085\tcharacteristic degree, ornaments, resting tones, colour, negative guidance.\n1086\t\n1087\t⬇\n1088\t\n1089\tmaqam bayati on D - scale: D E-half-flat F G A Bb C D,\n1090\t\n1091\tthe half-flat second degree (E koron) is the expressive soul\n1092\t\n1093\tof bayati, ornaments: shimmer on E-half-flat, slides\n1094\t\n1095\tD->E-half-flat, descending resolution G->F->E-half-flat->D,\n1096\t\n1097\tresting tones: D (tonic), G (dominant),\n1098\t\n1099\tcolor: melancholic, warm, yearning - the most expressive\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "810faa67-2c27-451c-a35b-94f5fe6397c3", "uuid": "60ab82e9-fc22-4da2-ac6d-ee09bc4a05d4", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T06:34:04.544Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mazzika_text.txt", "content": "audio_chunk messages tagged\nsource∈\\in{melody,percussion}.\n\nBackend and output\n\nA FastAPI server maintains a\nPlayerSession whose working memory is a rolling window of the last\n128128 note events (§4.2). A controller maps that state to an\naccompaniment behaviour and a generation-parameter vector, and the compiler\nsynthesizes the prompt (§4.5). Prompt and parameters are pushed\nto a live Lyria session whose background receive loop pulls 4848 kHz 1616-bit\nstereo PCM (Google DeepMind, 2025; Défossez et al., 2023), WAV-packs and base64-encodes\nit, and enqueues it on a bounded queue (maxsize=8=8, oldest dropped\nwhen full) so buffered audio cannot grow without bound. The client decodes each\nchunk with the Web Audio API and schedules playback on two separate\nAudioContexts using a running “next start” cursor with a small\nnegative overlap (−40-40 ms melody, −20-20 ms percussion) intended to crossfade\nchunk boundaries. The two AI streams are attenuated (0.45×0.45\\times melody,\n0.38×0.38\\times percussion) beneath the performer’s local piano, so arbitration\nbetween the voices is a deterministic gain decision rather than a generative\none.\n\n3.1 The Accompaniment State Machine\n\nThe controller resolves the estimated performance state into one of four\nbehaviours (Fig. 2). Supporting: while the\nsoloist plays, the band comps quietly beneath the line at the highest\nprompt-adherence and lowest density. Responding: when a phrase ends, an\nanswer window opens ∼0.3{\\sim}0.3–2.52.5 s afterward, in which the prompt lists\nthe soloist’s just-played pitches and instructs an expressive echo.\nSustain: a single note held for >2.5{>}2.5 s is read as a held tone and\nthe accompaniment stays minimal. LongIdle: after >5{>}5 s of silence\nwith sections active, the band assumes the floor and leads freely at the\nhighest density. Two gates bound the machine: a warmup gate requires two\nnotes after a section is conducted in before the ensemble may sound, and a\nsilence stop halts the melodic stream after >4{>}4 s of silence,\nforcing a fresh re-prompt when the player returns.\n\nSUPPORTINGcomp beneathRESPONDINGanswer phraseSUSTAINminimalLONG IDLEband leadsphrase ends(>>0.40.4 s)plays again1 note held>>2.52.5 snew notesilent>>55 splays(≥\\geq22 warmup)warmup gate:≥\\geq22 notesmelodic stream stops after >>44 s silence\nFigure 2: The four-state accompaniment policy π\\pi. Transitions are driven by\nplaying and silence timers over the performance stream; a warmup gate admits\nthe ensemble and a silence stop halts the melodic stream until the soloist\nresumes.\n\n3.2 Why Two Decoupled Streams\n\nMazzikaAI runs two independent Lyria sessions because melodic accompaniment\nand rhythmic bed obey different clocks. The melodic engine has\nmute_drums enabled and no fixed BPM lock; it is re-prompted as\nharmony, register and phrasing evolve. The percussion engine, once\ntriggered by gesture, runs continuously as a tempo-locked 4/44/4 bed at the\nselected BPM, independent of the soloist, cycling through escalating style\npatterns and using tighter generation parameters to stay literal and sparse.\nTheir control signals genuinely conflict: the melodic voice must be free to\nbreathe, slow, or rest to follow the performer, whereas a groove must remain\nmetronomically steady, and folding both into one prompt would force one to\ncompromise the other. Separation also isolates a hard model constraint—Lyria\ncannot change BPM on a live stream, so a tempo change forces a full\ndisconnect-and-reconnect, which the percussion engine can absorb without\ninterrupting the melodic conversation.\n\n4 Method: Compiling Performance into Prompts\n\n4.1 Problem Formulation\n\nWe treat companion accompaniment as a real-time transduction problem. Let the\nsoloist produce timestamped events e1,e2,…e_{1},e_{2},\\dots, each a MIDI note-on/off with\nvelocity, a sustain-pedal change, a gesture, or a control update. Rather than\ncondition the generator on the raw stream, the system maintains an estimated\nperformance state\n\nst=Φ​({ei:t−W≤τi≤t}),s_{t}=\\Phi\\!\\left(\\{e_{i}:t-W\\leq\\tau_{i}\\leq t\\}\\right),\n\nwhere the estimator Φ\\Phi (§4.2) runs over a bounded rolling\nwindow—a deque of the last 128128 note events, with feature-specific look-back\nhorizons WW of 22–3030 s. A discrete controller π\\pi maps the state to one\nof four accompaniment behaviours,\nqt=π​(st)∈{LongIdle,Responding,Supporting,Sustain}q_{t}=\\pi(s_{t})\\in\\{\\textsc{LongIdle},\\textsc{Responding},\\textsc{Supporting},\\textsc{Sustain}\\}, and Θ\\Theta maps (qt,st)(q_{t},s_{t}) to a genre-conditioned\ngeneration-parameter vector\nθt=(temperature,top-​k,guidance,density,brightness)\\theta_{t}=(\\text{temperature},\\text{top-}k,\\text{guidance},\\text{density},\\text{brightness}) (§4.3).\n\nThe method’s core is a deterministic prompt compiler\n\nC:(st,qt)⟼pt∈Σ∗,C:(s_{t},q_{t})\\;\\longmapsto\\;p_{t}\\in\\Sigma^{*},\n\na pure, side-effect-free function emitting a single natural-language string over\nthe alphabet Σ\\Sigma of English text (§4.5). Determinism is\nload-bearing: identical states yield identical prompts, which is what makes the\ndownstream gate sound. The generator GG—invoked as a streaming,\nweighted-prompt latent music model (Google DeepMind, 2025)—produces\nat=G​(pt,θt)a_{t}=G(p_{t},\\theta_{t}). Two instances of GG run concurrently\n(§3.2). To avoid destabilizing the live stream, compiler\noutput reaches GG only when a coarse control signature changes:\n\nre-prompt at ​t⇔σ​(st,qt,θt)≠σ​(st−,qt−,θt−).\\text{re-prompt at }t\\iff\\sigma(s_{t},q_{t},\\theta_{t})\\neq\\sigma(s_{t^{-}},q_{t^{-}},\\theta_{t^{-}}).\n\nState estimation, discrete control, deterministic text synthesis, gated\nstreaming: these four stages structure the rest of the section.\n\n4.2 Working Memory: Performance-State Estimation\n\nEstimation is driven by two inputs: MIDI events, which mutate state on arrival,\nand the 250250 ms heartbeat, which recomputes time-dependent features when no\nnote arrives. Table 2 lists the principal features and their\nhorizons.\n\nRegister is the mean active pitch (low<50{<}50, high>68{>}68,\nelse mid) and texture the number of simultaneously held keys. Over\nshort windows the estimator derives motion (sign of summed pitch differences of\nthe last five onsets), melodic centre and its direction, playing speed from\nnotes-per-second over 33 s, and a dynamic arc by comparing the early and late\nhalves of the last twelve velocities. Phrase segmentation is silence-driven:\ninter-onset gaps below 0.350.35 s are legato continuation, gaps above 0.40.4 s\nwhile silent mark a phrase end, and gaps above 2.52.5 s mark idle. A phrase end\nmore than 1.21.2 s after the previous one increments a verse counter that\nquantizes into a verse_stage (opening/developing/full), so the accompaniment opens up as the session matures. At each\ncounted phrase end the system captures last_phrase_pitches, the\nunique pitches of the just-ended phrase in order, so the ensemble can echo them\nliterally.\n\nFor harmonically grounded genres the estimator performs two independent forms of\nchord inference. Real-time recognition matches the pitch-class set of\ncurrently held keys against interval templates, trying every pitch class as\ncandidate root and scoring higher-information chords first (dominant/minor\nseventh >> triad >> power chord >> dyad >> octave), yielding a symbol such\nas A7 or Dm. When no chord is held, implied-chord\ninference estimates the current region of the twelve-bar blues form by scoring\nthe recent pitch-class histogram against the three functional harmonies, with\neach tonic weighted ×3\\times 3:\n\nscoreA7 (I)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{A7 (I)}}\n=3​#​(A)+#​(E)+#​(G),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(A)+\\#(E)+\\#(G),\n\nscoreD7 (IV)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{D7 (IV)}}\n=3​#​(D)+#​(A)+#​(C),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(D)+\\#(A)+\\#(C),\n\nscoreE7 (V)\\displaystyle\\text{score}_{\\text{E7 (V)}}\n=3​#​(E)+#​(B)+#​(D),\\displaystyle=3\\,\\#(E)+\\#(B)+\\#(D),\n\nreturning the arg-max region.\n\nTwo derived features exist purely to stabilize the downstream stream. The\nharmony fingerprint is a coarse pitch-class snapshot over the last\n22 s, quantized into 22-semitone buckets—tuple(sorted(set((p % 12)//2 for p in pitches)))—so that adjacent\nsemitones collapse and the fingerprint is meant to change only when harmony\ngenuinely shifts. It replaced an earlier key that changed on every note-on and\nre-prompted the model continuously during melodic runs; §6.2\nreports how far short of its intent it falls. The style profile is a\nnatural-language sentence recomputed every two verses from 3030 s of history\nusing genre-aware vocabulary, e.g. \"ascending blues runs climbing\ntoward the high note, wide bends and leaps, wailing in the high register\".\n\nTable 2: Working memory: principal features of the estimated performance state\nsts_{t}, with the horizon each is computed over.\n\nFeature\n\nHorizon\n\nValues / role\n\nTexture and dynamics\n\nregister\n\nheld keys\n\nlow / mid / high (mean pitch)\n\ntexture_hint\n\nheld keys\n\nsolo / duo / small / full chord\n\nmotion\n\n5 onsets\n\nascending / descending / static\n\nmelodic_center\n\n6 onsets\n\nmean pitch (register target)\n\nregister_follow\n\n6 onsets\n\nrise / fall / hold\n\nplaying_speed\n\n3 s\n\nfast / moderate / slow (notes s-1)\n\ndynamic_arc\n\n12 velocities\n\ncrescendo / diminuendo / steady\n\nPhrase and session time\n\nphrase_state\n\nsilence gap\n\nstart / continue / end / idle\n\nverse_count →\\to stage\n\nsession\n\nopening / developing / full\n\ncall_response_active\n\n0.3–2.5 s\n\npost-phrase answer window\n\nlong_idle\n\n>5{>}5 s\n\nplayer silent; band should lead\n\nis_sustained_hold\n\n>2.5{>}2.5 s\n\none note held\n\nlast_phrase_pitches\n\nphrase end\n\npitches to echo back\n\nHarmony\n\ndetected_chord\n\nheld keys\n\ntemplate-matched chord symbol\n\nimplied_chord\n\n2 s\n\n12-bar region (A7 / D7 / E7)\n\nrecent_pitch_context\n\nbuffer\n\nlast 10 unique note names\n\nharmony_fingerprint\n\n2 s\n\ncoarse PC buckets; gate input\n\nstyle_profile\n\n30 s\n\nNL sentence, recomputed / 2 verses\n\n4.3 Controller and Generation Parameters\n\nThe controller selects qtq_{t} by a simple interpretable priority\n(LongIdle >> Responding >> Supporting, with\nSustain as the special case of a single held note): the player actively\nplaying yields Supporting; the open answer window or an ending mode\nyields Responding; sustained silence with sections active yields\nLongIdle.\n\nEach state maps to a numeric control vector read from a per-genre table, which\nstores for each of the nine genres a\n(temperature,top-​k,guidance,density)(\\text{temperature},\\text{top-}k,\\text{guidance},\\text{density}) quadruple per\nstate plus three register-dependent brightness values—135135 hand-tuned values\nin total. Table 3 shows a representative slice. The rationale is\nconsistent across genres: higher temperature is more exploratory; lower\ntop-kk and higher guidance bind the model more tightly to the prompt; density\ntargets note sparsity. LongIdle therefore uses the highest temperature\nand density (the band improvises freely) while Supporting uses the\nlowest temperature and density and the highest guidance (the band stays\nliteral and out of the way). A verse-stage boost of 0.00/0.03/0.060.00/0.03/0.06 is added to\ndensity (capped at 0.900.90) so the accompaniment enriches gradually, and\nbrightness is selected by the soloist’s register so a lead voice cuts through in\nthe high register. The percussion engine uses markedly tighter settings (blues:\ntemperature 0.320.32, top-kk 88, guidance 10.510.5, density 0.320.32) so it plays\nthe pattern rather than improvising fills.\n\nTable 3: Representative generation parameters θt=Θ​(qt,st)\\theta_{t}=\\Theta(q_{t},s_{t}), four\nof nine genres. Density excludes the verse-stage boost; brightness is\nregister-dependent and omitted. The remaining knobs the API\nexposes—bpm (fixed at connect) and mute_drums (set for the\nmelodic engine)—are constant per engine.\n\nGenre\nState\ntemp\ntop-kk\nguid.\ndens.\n\nblues\nLongIdle\n0.78\n22\n5.5\n0.58\n\nResponding\n0.72\n18\n6.0\n0.42\n\nSupporting\n0.60\n12\n7.0\n0.22\n\nvintage arabic\nLongIdle\n0.72\n20\n5.8\n0.48\n\nResponding\n0.65\n16\n6.5\n0.38\n\nSupporting\n0.52\n12\n7.5\n0.20\n\njazz fusion\nLongIdle\n0.82\n24\n5.0\n0.55\n\nResponding\n0.74\n20\n5.8\n0.40\n\nSupporting\n0.64\n14\n6.8\n0.22\n\nclassical\nLongIdle\n0.60\n16\n6.5\n0.45\n\nResponding\n0.55\n14\n7.0\n0.32\n\nSupporting\n0.48\n10\n8.0\n0.18\n\nRe-prompt gating\n\nA live stream cannot tolerate re-prompting on every\nnote. The controller therefore emits a coarse control signature σ\\sigma—a\ntuple of only the musically meaningful determinants of the output: genre, maqam,\nthe LongIdle/Responding/answer-window flags, the harmony\nfingerprint rather than raw notes, the first four echo pitches, the\nactive section set, verse stage, density and brightness rounded into coarse\nbuckets, BPM, and the detected and implied chords. The transport layer pushes a\nnew prompt only when σ\\sigma changes. By design this makes the signature\ninvariant to the note-by-note churn of a melodic run and sensitive only to\ngenuine change; §6.2 measures how well that holds.\n\n4.4 The Knowledge Base\n\nEverything the system knows about music is hand-authored prose and numbers,\ninventoried in Table 4. Its core is the Arabic tradition: six\nmaqamat with quarter-tone spellings and a five-instrument takht\nconfiguration—cello, oud, strings, qanun, and nay (lead)—driven by a\nbespoke controller with idiom-specific harmonic and echo logic. Around that\ncore the same structures carry eight further Western genres (1515 mode\ndescriptions, 3838 ensemble sections with three role registers each, 135135\ngeneration parameters, and 3636 percussion patterns in total); the full genre\ncatalogue, which exists chiefly as evidence that retargeting costs only new\nprompt fragments, is given in Appendix A. The knowledge base\nis small enough to read in an afternoon.\n\nTable 4: Inventory of the hand-authored knowledge base. Every entry is\nhuman-readable text or a tuned constant; none is learned. Line counts are of the\ndeployed implementation.\n\nComponent\n\nSize\n\nContent and prompt slot\n\nMode / scale descriptions\n\n15\n\n6 Arabic maqamat (bayati, rast, hijaz, nahawand, saba, kurd) with\nquarter-tone spellings ++ 9 Western scales and modes; each gives note\nspelling, characteristic degree, ornaments, resting tones, colour, and\nnegative guidance →\\to maqam line\n\nEnsemble sections\n\n38\n\nacross 9 genres; each with a label and three role registers\n(supporting, responding, solo_responding),\ni.e. 114 role descriptions →\\to role assignment\n\nGenre configurations\n\n9\n\nsection order (low→\\tohigh), lead-preference list, header text,\nno-percussion clause (Appendix A, Table 8)\n\nGeneration parameters\n\n135\n\n99 genres ×\\times (33 states ×\\times 44 knobs ++ 33 brightness\nvalues) →\\to θt\\theta_{t} (Table 3)\n\nPercussion patterns\n\n36\n\n99 genre families ×\\times 44 escalating styles, each with a tempo anchor\nand a no-melody exclusion clause\n\nHarmonic rules\n\n—\n\ninterval-template chord recognition, 12-bar region inference, four-tier\nblues harmonic-lock priority\n\nImplementation\n\n2,4342{,}434 lines of compiler,\n246246 of controller, 571571 of state estimation (Python)\n\nMaqam grounding\n\nTo hold a general model inside a modal world it was\nnot trained to privilege, the compiler injects a dense scale description. The\ncanonical entry is Listing 1.\n\nListing 1: The bayati grounding clause, verbatim from the\nknowledge base. All 15 mode entries follow this template: spelling,\ncharacteristic degree, ornaments, resting tones, colour, negative guidance.\n\n⬇\n\nmaqam bayati on D - scale: D E-half-flat F G A Bb C D,\n\nthe half-flat second degree (E koron) is the expressive soul\n\nof bayati, ornaments: shimmer on E-half-flat, slides\n\nD->E-half-flat, descending resolution G->F->E-half-flat->D,\n\nresting tones: D (tonic), G (dominant),\n\ncolor: melancholic, warm, yearning - the most expressive", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 600, "totalLines": 2437}}}
