{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a042099941b15f47", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1100\t\n1101\tArabic maqam, avoid western major or minor tonality,\n1102\t\n1103\tstay in bayati modal world\n1104\t\n1105\tTwo choices matter. The microtonal degree is spelled phonetically\n1106\t(“E-half-flat”, “E koron”) rather than symbolically, because the model\n1107\tconsumes prose, not notation. And the closing clause supplies explicit negative\n1108\tguidance—the prose analogue of a repulsion term, intended to keep the latent\n1109\ttrajectory inside the mode (Liu et al., 2023; Brown et al., 2020).\n1110\t\n1111\t4.5 Prompt Synthesis\n1112\t\n1113\tThe compiler assembles ptp_{t} as an ordered concatenation of clauses\n1114\tjoined by commas. Ordering is load-bearing—hard constraints are placed first,\n1115\tbefore any evocative description:\n1116\t\n1117\tpt=\\displaystyle p_{t}=\n1118\t[instrument rule / silence]∥[no-percussion]\\displaystyle[\\text{instrument rule / silence}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{no-percussion}]\n1119\t\n1120\t∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n1121\t[specific header]​‖[session stage]‖​[maqam line]\\displaystyle[\\text{specific header}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{session stage}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{maqam line}]\n1122\t\n1123\t∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n1124\t[harmonic context / echo]∥[role assignment]\\displaystyle[\\text{harmonic context / echo}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{role assignment}]\n1125\t\n1126\t∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n1127\t[register]​‖[dynamics]‖​[space clause].\\displaystyle[\\text{register}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{dynamics}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{space clause}].\n1128\t\n1129\tInstrument-constraint control\n1130\t\n1131\tBecause the player conducts a subset\n1132\tof instruments in, the prompt must actively suppress the rest—a\n1133\tprompt-level substitute for the stem-level control the API does not expose. The\n1134\tleading clause names only the active instruments and issues a hard silence\n1135\tdirective for every inactive one (Listing 2, top). Placing it first\n1136\tmeans the model reads the mixing constraint before it ever reads “slow delta\n1137\tblues”.\n1138\t\n1139\tRole layering and echo\n1140\t\n1141\tEach section carries three role registers, so\n1142\tthe same instrument reads differently comping and soloing. The controller picks\n1143\tthe lead from a per-genre preference list (blues: harmonica >> lead guitar >>\n1144\tpiano) and assigns the remainder their supporting text. In\n1145\tResponding the lead receives its responding text and the rest\n1146\thold; in LongIdle the lead receives the richer\n1147\tsolo_responding text. The most direct expression of interaction is a\n1148\tliteral echo: the compiler converts last_phrase_pitches to note names\n1149\tand instructs an answer (Listing 2, bottom). The blues path\n1150\tadditionally selects harmonic guidance by a strict four-tier\n1151\tpriority—a chord held right now triggers a hard chord lock; otherwise the\n1152\tinferred twelve-bar region; otherwise the recent pitch context; otherwise the\n1153\traw active notes—so the band always comps on the most reliable signal\n1154\tavailable.\n1155\t\n1156\tListing 2: Two prompt-level control mechanisms that substitute\n1157\tfor control the model API does not expose: instrument suppression in place of\n1158\tstem-level mixing (top), and a phrase echo that turns transcription into\n1159\tturn-taking (bottom, blues path).\n1160\t\n1161\t⬇\n1162\t\n1163\tINSTRUMENT RULE - this track contains ONLY: <active>.\n1164\t\n1165\tThe following produce absolutely no audio and must not appear:\n1166\t\n1167\t<Harmonica: NO SOUND, Lead Guitar: NO SOUND, ...>.\n1168\t\n1169\tGenerate nothing for those instruments.\n1170\t\n1171\t%\n1172\t\n1173\tsoloist just played: A -> C -> E -\n1174\t\n1175\ttheir pitch world this session: A -> C -> D -> E -> G -\n1176\t\n1177\tanswer using those same pitches with blues character:\n1178\t\n1179\tslide into the first note, bend with blues phrasing,\n1180\t\n1181\tresolve back to A - ’I heard you, here is my reply’\n1182\t\n1183\tA compiled prompt\n1184\t\n1185\tListing 3 shows one\n1186\tcomplete melodic prompt as assembled for a concrete situation: genre blues; bass,\n1187\tpiano and harmonica conducted in; state Responding; the player has just\n1188\tplayed A3 →\\to C4 →\\to E4. Every mechanism above appears in the\n1189\tfixed clause order. It also exposes a defect: the residual scale:\n1190\tbayati clause is the maqam field, which the blues path does not clear, leaking\n1191\tinto a blues prompt. We report it rather than repair the example, because it\n1192\tillustrates the practical value of a text interface—a compiler bug is legible\n1193\tas a stray English phrase in the artifact the model receives, with no\n1194\tinstrumentation required.\n1195\t\n1196\tListing 3: A complete compiled melodic prompt (blues,\n1197\tResponding, harmonica lead), verbatim.\n1198\t\n1199\t⬇\n1200\t\n1201\tINSTRUMENT RULE - this track contains ONLY: Bass, Piano, Harmonica.\n1202\t\n1203\tThe following produce absolutely no audio and must not appear:\n1204\t\n1205\tRhythm: NO SOUND, Lead Guitar: NO SOUND. Generate nothing for\n1206\t\n1207\tthose instruments., no drums, no drum kit, no hi-hat, no snare,\n1208\t\n1209\tno kick drum, Bass, Piano and Harmonica, slow delta blues style\n1210\t\n1211\tin A minor, A blues hexatonic scale (A C D Eb E G) - the b3 (C)\n1212\t\n1213\tand b5 (Eb) are the expressive blue notes, bends up into C and slides\n1214\t\n1215\tfrom Eb to E are the signature blues moves, slow and laid-back blues\n1216\t\n1217\tfeel, session building - finding the groove, getting warmer,\n1218\t\n1219\tscale: bayati, expressive and authentic character, soloist just\n1220\t\n1221\tplayed: A3 -> C4 -> E4 - answer with a direct blues response:\n1222\t\n1223\tslide into the first note, bend through A3 -> C4 -> E4 with blues\n1224\t\n1225\tphrasing, add vibrato on the held notes, resolve back to A - it must\n1226\t\n1227\tsound like ’I heard you, here is my reply’, Harmonica steps forward -\n1228\t\n1229\tplays a short expressive blues lick, wailing bends and slides, raw\n1230\t\n1231\tblues harp phrasing, intimate and soulful - a direct musical answer\n1232\t\n1233\tto the soloist - others hold the groove underneath: Bass: electric\n1234\t\n1235\tbass holds deep root notes, barely moving, quiet resonant foundation\n1236\t\n1237\tunder the soloist; Piano: piano holds quiet sustained chords, soft\n1238\t\n1239\tand warm, minimal movement - a harmonic cushion behind the soloist,\n1240\t\n1241\tmid-energy soulful blues answer, groove is finding itself - confident\n1242\t\n1243\tbut not yet at full peak, slow and soulful - play each phrase with\n1244\t\n1245\tlong sustained notes, leave space and silence after each answer\n1246\t\n1247\tThis single string, regenerated only when σ\\sigma changes and paired with\n1248\tθt\\theta_{t}, is the entirety of what MazzikaAI sends to the generator.\n1249\t\n1250\t5 Implementation\n1251\t\n1252\tThe client is a React 19/TypeScript single-page application bundled by Vite; all\n1253\taudio, input and vision capabilities come from native browser APIs. The backend\n1254\tis a FastAPI service served by Uvicorn, depending on google-genai for\n1255\tboth Lyria RealTime generation and Gemini vision (optical music recognition in\n1256\tLearn Mode) and on NumPy and soundfile for audio packing. Every hot-path\n1257\tmessage travels over a single persistent WebSocket, keeping per-event overhead\n1258\tlow and connection setup off the latency path.\n1259\t\n1260\tStreaming engine\n1261\t\n1262\tThe generative model is encapsulated in an\n1263\tasynchronous LyriaEngine wrapping one live music session against the\n1264\tmodels/lyria-realtime-exp endpoint. It exposes a small idempotent\n1265\tsurface: update_controls() pushes a weighted prompt\n1266\t[(text,1.0)][(\\text{text},1.0)] and a generation config, and caches both so each is\n1267\tre-sent only when it actually changes; play() and stop() gate\n1268\tthe stream. A background receive loop packs each incoming 4848 kHz stereo PCM\n1269\tchunk into a WAV container, base64-encodes it, and enqueues it on a bounded\n1270\tqueue, dropping the oldest chunk when full—trading a dropped frame under load\n1271\tfor a hard cap on end-to-end latency. Stopping an engine drains the queue so\n1272\tstale audio never plays after the band has been silenced. The engine treats a\n1273\tnormally-completed receive stream and a ConnectionClosed/APIError identically, clearing the session handle so the next control\n1274\ttick reconnects transparently; transient drops and server-side stream expiry are\n1275\ttherefore invisible to the performer.\n1276\t\n1277\tTwo-engine orchestration\n1278\t\n1279\tOn every inbound message—and on each\n1280\t250250 ms tick—the backend loop recomputes the shared state and then advances\n1281\teach engine under its own gating logic. The melodic engine sounds only once at\n1282\tleast one section is conducted in and the warmup gate has passed; it stays active\n1283\twhile the soloist plays or within four seconds of the last note, after which it\n1284\tstops and resets its control signature so a fresh prompt is issued on return. The\n1285\tpercussion engine is armed by a flag raised by gesture or voice and then plays\n1286\tcontinuously until stopped; its signature is simply\n1287\t(bpm,style index)(\\textsc{bpm},\\text{style index}). A BPM change on either engine forces a\n1288\tdisconnect-and-reconnect, and a session detected as “triggered but not\n1289\tplaying” is reset and restarted.\n1290\t\n1291\tClient-side audio\n1292\t\n1293\tThe browser renders the two streams on two\n1294\tseparate AudioContext instances so they do not contend on one\n1295\tscheduler. Each chunk is base64-decoded and turned into an AudioBuffer\n1296\tvia decodeAudioData. Playback is scheduled with a running “next start”\n1297\tcursor: each buffer starts at max(currentTime, nextStart) and the cursor\n1298\tadvances by the buffer duration minus a negative overlap (4040 ms melody,\n1299\t2020 ms percussion) intended to crossfade chunk boundaries and hide decoder edge\n1300\tartifacts. To bound latency after a pause, each scheduler resets its cursor when\n1301\tthe wall-clock gap since the previous chunk exceeds a threshold (600600 ms\n1302\tmelody, 800800 ms percussion); without the reset a new chunk would be scheduled\n1303\tat a stale future timestamp, producing an audible silent delay.\n1304\tBecause the model’s steady-state cadence is ∼2{\\sim}2 s, the reset path\n1305\trather than the crossfade cursor dominates in steady state\n1306\t(§6.2).\n1307\t\n1308\tMultimodal control\n1309\t\n1310\tFour modalities funnel into the same session\n1311\tstate. MIDI: note-on, note-off and CC64 sustain are decoded per input\n1312\tdevice and forwarded with note, velocity, event type and timestamp.\n1313\tGesture: a MediaPipe Hands (Lugaresi et al., 2019) pipeline\n1314\t(maxNumHands=2=2, confidence 0.70.7) infers per-finger extension from\n1315\tlandmark ordering and recognizes two robust static poses—index-only toggles\n1316\tpercussion, open palm toggles the ensemble. Each must be held for 700700 ms\n1317\tbefore acting, followed by a 22–33 s cooldown, with hold progress surfaced as\n1318\ta visual cursor. Voice: continuous Web Speech recognition, gated by\n1319\tconfidence ≥0.62\\geq 0.62 and at most eight words, parsed against a rule set\n1320\trequiring an explicit action word paired with a target; a 1.51.5 s deduplication\n1321\twindow prevents double firing. Learn Mode: MusicXML and .mxl are\n1322\tparsed entirely in-browser; PDF scores are rendered to page images with PDF.js\n1323\tand sent to Gemini vision, which returns a structured note list, with model\n1324\tfallbacks tried newest-first.\n1325\t\n1326\tConstraints that shaped the design\n1327\t\n1328\tFour properties of the deployed\n1329\tgenerator determined non-obvious choices. Lyria cannot change tempo on an open\n1330\tstream, so BPM changes are implemented as reconnects and the percussion bed is\n1331\ttempo-locked by design. The API exposes no stem-level mixing, so instrument\n1332\tcontrol is prompt-level and best-effort. Re-prompting on every note caused\n1333\taudible thrashing, motivating the fingerprint-based gate. And the model has no\n1334\tnotion of the human’s own sound, so keeping the AI behind the soloist is done\n1335\twith client-side attenuation plus explicit space-enforcing clauses in the prompt.\n1336\tThe recurring theme is that fine-grained control over a general model is\n1337\tobtained without touching weights: the levers are prompt design,\n1338\tgeneration-config scheduling, connection-lifecycle management, and client-side\n1339\tmixing.\n1340\t\n1341\t6 Evaluation\n1342\t\n1343\tWe evaluate MazzikaAI along three lines: (i) instrumented measurement of\n1344\tthe deployed pipeline—latency, throughput, and stream stability\n1345\t(§6.1–§6.2); (ii) controlled\n1346\tablations that attribute output behaviour to specific design choices over\n1347\tinput-identical replayed sessions (§6.4); and (iii) a\n1348\tperceptual evaluation in which two expert musicians played the deployed\n1349\tsystem (§6.3). The pipeline measurements come from a live\n1350\tblues session—latency, gating, and stability are properties of the\n1351\ttransport and control loop, not of any genre’s prompt content—while all\n1352\toutput-quality experiments target the system’s design centre, Arabic\n1353\tmaqam.\n1354\t\n1355\tApparatus and method\n1356\t\n1357\tThe setup mirrors the deployment of\n1358\t§5: a MIDI keyboard driving the front end in Chromium,\n1359\tthe FastAPI backend on the same host, and Lyria RealTime (Google DeepMind, 2025) over\n1360\tthe public API. We instrumented every pipeline boundary—key capture, WebSocket\n1361\tsend, backend receive, compile completion, prompt push, chunk arrival, client\n1362\tdecode, scheduled start—as a timestamped JSONL timeline. Because both processes\n1363\tshare a host, client and server wall clocks are directly comparable across the\n1364\tboundary; intra-process durations use the monotonic clock. Per-stage figures are\n1365\tdifferences between adjacent timestamps. Because a real-time system must be\n1366\tjudged by its worst cases as well as its typical case, each distribution is\n1367\tsummarized by its median together with its 95th and 99th percentiles (p95 and\n1368\tp99)—the values below which 95%95\\% and 99%99\\% of observations fall,\n1369\tcomputed by the nearest-rank method. The end-to-end\n1370\tfigure is measured directly rather than summed: when a re-prompt fires, the\n1371\tbackend attaches the client wall-clock timestamp of the causal key press to every\n1372\tchunk generated under the new prompt, and the client computes the delay from that\n1373\tpress to the moment the first such chunk is scheduled to become audible. We\n1374\treport a primary session of 8.58.5 min of continuous playing (1,5161{,}516 note\n1375\tevents, 758758 onsets, both engines active, 352352 chunks delivered), consistent\n1376\twith a shorter preliminary session whose figures agree within noise.\n1377\t\n1378\t6.1 Latency and Throughput\n1379\t\n1380\tFigure 3: Cost of every pipeline stage over the primary 8.58.5 min session: dot\n1381\tis the median, the solid bar extends to p95 and the faint bar to p99. The three\n1382\tknowledge-based stages (upper band) together cost under a millisecond even at\n1383\tthe tail; the generative model and its chunked delivery (lower band) sit three\n1384\torders of magnitude higher. Sub-0.10.1 ms values are drawn at the 0.10.1 ms\n1385\taxis floor.\n1386\t\n1387\tFigure 4: (a) Empirical CDF of directly measured key-press-to-audible latency\n1388\tagainst the 0.30.3–2.52.5 s answer window the controller itself opens. The\n1389\tnote-triggered median of 263263 ms lands at the front of the window and the p99\n1390\tof 945945 ms well inside it; the heavier “all re-prompts” tail includes\n1391\theartbeat-fired re-prompts, whose elapsed time from the anchoring key press\n1392\tincludes musical waiting rather than pipeline delay. (b) Chunk inter-arrival\n1393\ttimes for both engines, concentrated at the model’s 2.02.0 s chunk—generation\n1394\truns at 1×1\\times real time. Gaps ≥5{\\geq}5 s, which span playing pauses and\n1395\tstream restarts rather than steady-state delivery, are excluded (44 of 146146\n1396\tmelody and 22 of 203203 percussion gaps).\n1397\t\n1398\tFigures 3–4 and Table 5\n1399\treport the measured decomposition. Three observations follow.\n1400\t\n1401\tFirst, the knowledge-based layer is essentially free. State update plus\n1402\tprompt compilation totals roughly half a millisecond even at p99, and the push\n1403\tAPI round trip is sub-millisecond (Fig. 3); the entire budget is\n1404\tspent in the generative model and its chunked delivery, which emits uniform\n1405\t2.02.0 s chunks at a ∼2.0{\\sim}2.0 s cadence\n1406\t(Fig. 4b). This cleanly separates the\n1407\tcontribution’s cost from the backend model’s: a faster generator would improve\n1408\tresponsiveness without touching the compiler, and the compiler could run\n1409\tcomfortably inside a far tighter budget than the one it currently sits in.\n1410\t\n1411\tSecond, the direct end-to-end median of 263263 ms for note-triggered re-prompts\n1412\tsits at the front of the 0.30.3–2.52.5 s answer window encoded in the session\n1413\tlogic. This is the sense in which turn-taking buys latency headroom: an\n1414\taccompanist’s reply is musically expected to arrive somewhat after the\n1415\tsoloist stops, so delay that would be unacceptable for beat-synchronous playing\n1416\tis perceptually absorbed by the response gap. Three implementation choices bound\n1417\tthe tail deliberately—the bounded queue that drops the oldest chunk rather than\n1418\taccumulating backlog, the negative scheduling overlap, and the re-prompt gate\n1419\tthat removes per-note compile and API round trips from the hot path. We stress\n1420\tthat fitting the reaction budget does not produce beat-level entrainment,\n1421\ta distinction the expert playing sessions draw sharply (§6.3).\n1422\t\n1423\tThird, the percussion engine’s much larger push-to-chunk figure\n1424\t(median 1,1601{,}160 ms, p99 ≈12{\\approx}12 s) reflects its lifecycle rather than\n1425\tits steady state: percussion pushes cluster at style changes and BPM-change\n1426\treconnects, where the stream must be re-established before audio resumes.\n1427\t\n1428\tTable 5: Measured latency decomposition, primary session (8.58.5 min,\n1429\t1,5161{,}516 note events). All values in ms, nearest-rank percentiles. The\n1430\t“push→\\tonext chunk” rows report arrival of the first chunk after the new\n1431\tprompt—a lower bound on when audibly affected audio arrives, since chunk\n1432\tcontent is not attributable to a prompt without audio analysis. The\n1433\tchunk-cadence rows are inter-arrival gaps between an engine’s consecutive\n1434\tserver-side emission records (147147 melody, 204204 percussion;\n1435\tTable 6), excluding the six gaps ≥5{\\geq}5 s that span\n1436\tplaying pauses and stream restarts rather than steady-state delivery (44\n1437\tmelody, 22 percussion), hence n=142n{=}142 of 146146 and n=201n{=}201 of 203203\n1438\tgaps. The percussion engine is soloist-decoupled by design and so has no\n1439\tend-to-end row.\n1440\t\n1441\tStage\n1442\tmedian\n1443\tp95\n1444\tp99\n1445\tnn\n1446\t\n1447\tKnowledge-based layer\n1448\t\n1449\tBackend state update\n1450\t0.30.3\n1451\t0.50.5\n1452\t0.60.6\n1453\t3,5553{,}555\n1454\t\n1455\tPrompt compile\n1456\t0.20.2\n1457\t0.30.3\n1458\t0.40.4\n1459\t3,5553{,}555\n1460\t\n1461\tRe-prompt push (API round trip)\n1462\t0.30.3\n1463\t0.80.8\n1464\t0.90.9\n1465\t1,4011{,}401\n1466\t\n1467\tTransport and client\n1468\t\n1469\tKey capture →\\rightarrow WS send\n1470\t\n1471\t<1<\\!1\n1472\t11\n1473\t22\n1474\t1,5161{,}516\n1475\t\n1476\tWS transport up (note events)\n1477\t1.31.3\n1478\t8.78.7\n1479\t24.224.2\n1480\t1,5161{,}516\n1481\t\n1482\tWS transport down (chunks)\n1483\t33.933.9\n1484\t40.840.8\n1485\t44.144.1\n1486\t352352\n1487\t\n1488\tClient decode ++ schedule\n1489\t\n1490\t3.13.1\n1491\t5.55.5\n1492\t9.09.0\n1493\t352352\n1494\t\n1495\tGenerative model\n1496\t\n1497\tPush →\\rightarrow next chunk (melody)\n1498\t\n1499\t150150\n1500\t1,0701{,}070\n1501\t1,7991{,}799\n1502\t140140\n1503\t\n1504\tPush →\\rightarrow next chunk (perc.)\n1505\t\n1506\t1,1601{,}160\n1507\t3,2503{,}250\n1508\t11,93411{,}934\n1509\t3030\n1510\t\n1511\tChunk cadence (melody)\n1512\t1,9991{,}999\n1513\t2,9782{,}978\n1514\t3,4593{,}459\n1515\t142142\n1516\t\n1517\tChunk cadence (percussion)\n1518\t2,0242{,}024\n1519\t2,9812{,}981\n1520\t3,4823{,}482\n1521\t201201\n1522\t\n1523\tEnd-to-end (directly measured)\n1524\t\n1525\tNote-triggered re-prompts\n1526\t263263\n1527\t818818\n1528\t945945\n1529\t9898\n1530\t\n1531\tAll re-prompts\n1532\t358358\n1533\t2,3342{,}334\n1534\t5,3555{,}355\n1535\t140140\n1536\t\n1537\t6.2 Gating and Stream Stability\n1538\t\n1539\tFigure 5: (a) Gate decisions by driver, split into re-prompts fired and decisions\n1540\tabsorbed, with the absorbed percentage above each bar: the gate absorbs 74%74\\%\n1541\tof heartbeat-driven decisions and 15%15\\% of note-driven ones. (b)\n1542\tRe-steering rate against note-onset rate in 1010 s bins: re-prompts track\n1543\tplaying activity closely and average 179179 per minute, with zero queue drops,\n1544\treconnects, or stream failures across the session.\n1545\t\n1546\tThe central result of this subsection is one of stability: the\n1547\tpipeline tolerates an order of magnitude more re-steering than its design\n1548\tassumed. Over the primary session, 1,5171{,}517 gate fires (179179 per\n1549\tminute—roughly one re-steer per generated 22 s chunk) reached the engines\n1550\tas 1,4011{,}401 API pushes (166166 per minute), and the stream exhibited zero\n1551\tfailures: no reconnects, no stream terminations, and no bounded-queue drops,\n1552\twith delivery continuing at the model’s native cadence throughout\n1553\t(Fig. 5b, Table 6). The practical ceiling on\n1554\tre-prompt frequency is therefore set by musical coherence—how often the\n1555\tmodel should be re-steered—rather than by transport or model\n1556\tstability. We scope the claim precisely: two service-side disconnects\n1557\t(1011 service unavailable) occurred in the same log, at 631631 s and\n1558\t684684 s—after playing had stopped, during an idle tail in which the\n1559\tbrowser was left connected. Both were handled by the engine’s self-healing\n1560\tpath; they bear on idle-session lifetime, not on stability under load.\n1561\t\n1562\tThe gate itself absorbed 43.1%43.1\\% of the 2,6652{,}665 decisions it evaluated:\n1563\t74.4%74.4\\% of heartbeat-driven decisions and 15.1%15.1\\% of note-driven ones\n1564\t(Fig. 5a). The lower note-driven selectivity traces to the\n1565\tharmony fingerprint, whose 22-semitone pitch-class buckets over a 22 s\n1566\twindow are fine enough that most new melodic pitches change the bucket set\n1567\tduring active playing. The fingerprint’s granularity is a tunable parameter,\n1568\tand coarsening it—or adding hysteresis—is a directly measurable\n1569\trefinement (§7.4). On the client side, chunk-boundary\n1570\tcontinuity in steady state rests chiefly on the regularity of chunk arrival\n1571\t(Fig. 4b); the crossfade cursor of\n1572\t§5 engages mainly during burst deliveries, and\n1573\tre-anchoring its reset condition to the scheduled playback horizon rather\n1574\tthan the arrival gap is a mechanical refinement.\n1575\t\n1576\tTable 6: Measured gating and streaming-stability summary, primary session.\n1577\tFailure counters are for the 8.58.5 min active window; the two service-side\n1578\tdisconnects in the same log occurred later, in the idle tail\n1579\t(§6.2). The per-engine chunk split is taken from client-side\n1580\tdelivery records; the server-side emission log captured 351351 of the 352352\n1581\tdeliveries (147147 melody, 204204 percussion), one melody log write having been\n1582\tdropped at a stream boundary.\n1583\t\n1584\tQuantity\n1585\t\n1586\tValue\n1587\t\n1588\tSession length / note events / onsets\n1589\t\n1590\t8.58.5 min / 1,5161{,}516 / 758758\n1591\t\n1592\tGate decisions evaluated\n1593\t\n1594\t2,6652{,}665\n1595\t\n1596\t  absorbed, all drivers\n1597\t\n1598\t1,1481{,}148 (43.1%43.1\\%)\n1599\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "810faa67-2c27-451c-a35b-94f5fe6397c3", "uuid": "7f3ea335-43ec-4112-bb3d-fd8ed6f52311", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T06:34:18.434Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mazzika_text.txt", "content": "\nArabic maqam, avoid western major or minor tonality,\n\nstay in bayati modal world\n\nTwo choices matter. The microtonal degree is spelled phonetically\n(“E-half-flat”, “E koron”) rather than symbolically, because the model\nconsumes prose, not notation. And the closing clause supplies explicit negative\nguidance—the prose analogue of a repulsion term, intended to keep the latent\ntrajectory inside the mode (Liu et al., 2023; Brown et al., 2020).\n\n4.5 Prompt Synthesis\n\nThe compiler assembles ptp_{t} as an ordered concatenation of clauses\njoined by commas. Ordering is load-bearing—hard constraints are placed first,\nbefore any evocative description:\n\npt=\\displaystyle p_{t}=\n[instrument rule / silence]∥[no-percussion]\\displaystyle[\\text{instrument rule / silence}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{no-percussion}]\n\n∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n[specific header]​‖[session stage]‖​[maqam line]\\displaystyle[\\text{specific header}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{session stage}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{maqam line}]\n\n∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n[harmonic context / echo]∥[role assignment]\\displaystyle[\\text{harmonic context / echo}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{role assignment}]\n\n∥\\displaystyle\\,\\|\n[register]​‖[dynamics]‖​[space clause].\\displaystyle[\\text{register}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{dynamics}]\\,\\|\\,[\\text{space clause}].\n\nInstrument-constraint control\n\nBecause the player conducts a subset\nof instruments in, the prompt must actively suppress the rest—a\nprompt-level substitute for the stem-level control the API does not expose. The\nleading clause names only the active instruments and issues a hard silence\ndirective for every inactive one (Listing 2, top). Placing it first\nmeans the model reads the mixing constraint before it ever reads “slow delta\nblues”.\n\nRole layering and echo\n\nEach section carries three role registers, so\nthe same instrument reads differently comping and soloing. The controller picks\nthe lead from a per-genre preference list (blues: harmonica >> lead guitar >>\npiano) and assigns the remainder their supporting text. In\nResponding the lead receives its responding text and the rest\nhold; in LongIdle the lead receives the richer\nsolo_responding text. The most direct expression of interaction is a\nliteral echo: the compiler converts last_phrase_pitches to note names\nand instructs an answer (Listing 2, bottom). The blues path\nadditionally selects harmonic guidance by a strict four-tier\npriority—a chord held right now triggers a hard chord lock; otherwise the\ninferred twelve-bar region; otherwise the recent pitch context; otherwise the\nraw active notes—so the band always comps on the most reliable signal\navailable.\n\nListing 2: Two prompt-level control mechanisms that substitute\nfor control the model API does not expose: instrument suppression in place of\nstem-level mixing (top), and a phrase echo that turns transcription into\nturn-taking (bottom, blues path).\n\n⬇\n\nINSTRUMENT RULE - this track contains ONLY: <active>.\n\nThe following produce absolutely no audio and must not appear:\n\n<Harmonica: NO SOUND, Lead Guitar: NO SOUND, ...>.\n\nGenerate nothing for those instruments.\n\n%\n\nsoloist just played: A -> C -> E -\n\ntheir pitch world this session: A -> C -> D -> E -> G -\n\nanswer using those same pitches with blues character:\n\nslide into the first note, bend with blues phrasing,\n\nresolve back to A - ’I heard you, here is my reply’\n\nA compiled prompt\n\nListing 3 shows one\ncomplete melodic prompt as assembled for a concrete situation: genre blues; bass,\npiano and harmonica conducted in; state Responding; the player has just\nplayed A3 →\\to C4 →\\to E4. Every mechanism above appears in the\nfixed clause order. It also exposes a defect: the residual scale:\nbayati clause is the maqam field, which the blues path does not clear, leaking\ninto a blues prompt. We report it rather than repair the example, because it\nillustrates the practical value of a text interface—a compiler bug is legible\nas a stray English phrase in the artifact the model receives, with no\ninstrumentation required.\n\nListing 3: A complete compiled melodic prompt (blues,\nResponding, harmonica lead), verbatim.\n\n⬇\n\nINSTRUMENT RULE - this track contains ONLY: Bass, Piano, Harmonica.\n\nThe following produce absolutely no audio and must not appear:\n\nRhythm: NO SOUND, Lead Guitar: NO SOUND. Generate nothing for\n\nthose instruments., no drums, no drum kit, no hi-hat, no snare,\n\nno kick drum, Bass, Piano and Harmonica, slow delta blues style\n\nin A minor, A blues hexatonic scale (A C D Eb E G) - the b3 (C)\n\nand b5 (Eb) are the expressive blue notes, bends up into C and slides\n\nfrom Eb to E are the signature blues moves, slow and laid-back blues\n\nfeel, session building - finding the groove, getting warmer,\n\nscale: bayati, expressive and authentic character, soloist just\n\nplayed: A3 -> C4 -> E4 - answer with a direct blues response:\n\nslide into the first note, bend through A3 -> C4 -> E4 with blues\n\nphrasing, add vibrato on the held notes, resolve back to A - it must\n\nsound like ’I heard you, here is my reply’, Harmonica steps forward -\n\nplays a short expressive blues lick, wailing bends and slides, raw\n\nblues harp phrasing, intimate and soulful - a direct musical answer\n\nto the soloist - others hold the groove underneath: Bass: electric\n\nbass holds deep root notes, barely moving, quiet resonant foundation\n\nunder the soloist; Piano: piano holds quiet sustained chords, soft\n\nand warm, minimal movement - a harmonic cushion behind the soloist,\n\nmid-energy soulful blues answer, groove is finding itself - confident\n\nbut not yet at full peak, slow and soulful - play each phrase with\n\nlong sustained notes, leave space and silence after each answer\n\nThis single string, regenerated only when σ\\sigma changes and paired with\nθt\\theta_{t}, is the entirety of what MazzikaAI sends to the generator.\n\n5 Implementation\n\nThe client is a React 19/TypeScript single-page application bundled by Vite; all\naudio, input and vision capabilities come from native browser APIs. The backend\nis a FastAPI service served by Uvicorn, depending on google-genai for\nboth Lyria RealTime generation and Gemini vision (optical music recognition in\nLearn Mode) and on NumPy and soundfile for audio packing. Every hot-path\nmessage travels over a single persistent WebSocket, keeping per-event overhead\nlow and connection setup off the latency path.\n\nStreaming engine\n\nThe generative model is encapsulated in an\nasynchronous LyriaEngine wrapping one live music session against the\nmodels/lyria-realtime-exp endpoint. It exposes a small idempotent\nsurface: update_controls() pushes a weighted prompt\n[(text,1.0)][(\\text{text},1.0)] and a generation config, and caches both so each is\nre-sent only when it actually changes; play() and stop() gate\nthe stream. A background receive loop packs each incoming 4848 kHz stereo PCM\nchunk into a WAV container, base64-encodes it, and enqueues it on a bounded\nqueue, dropping the oldest chunk when full—trading a dropped frame under load\nfor a hard cap on end-to-end latency. Stopping an engine drains the queue so\nstale audio never plays after the band has been silenced. The engine treats a\nnormally-completed receive stream and a ConnectionClosed/APIError identically, clearing the session handle so the next control\ntick reconnects transparently; transient drops and server-side stream expiry are\ntherefore invisible to the performer.\n\nTwo-engine orchestration\n\nOn every inbound message—and on each\n250250 ms tick—the backend loop recomputes the shared state and then advances\neach engine under its own gating logic. The melodic engine sounds only once at\nleast one section is conducted in and the warmup gate has passed; it stays active\nwhile the soloist plays or within four seconds of the last note, after which it\nstops and resets its control signature so a fresh prompt is issued on return. The\npercussion engine is armed by a flag raised by gesture or voice and then plays\ncontinuously until stopped; its signature is simply\n(bpm,style index)(\\textsc{bpm},\\text{style index}). A BPM change on either engine forces a\ndisconnect-and-reconnect, and a session detected as “triggered but not\nplaying” is reset and restarted.\n\nClient-side audio\n\nThe browser renders the two streams on two\nseparate AudioContext instances so they do not contend on one\nscheduler. Each chunk is base64-decoded and turned into an AudioBuffer\nvia decodeAudioData. Playback is scheduled with a running “next start”\ncursor: each buffer starts at max(currentTime, nextStart) and the cursor\nadvances by the buffer duration minus a negative overlap (4040 ms melody,\n2020 ms percussion) intended to crossfade chunk boundaries and hide decoder edge\nartifacts. To bound latency after a pause, each scheduler resets its cursor when\nthe wall-clock gap since the previous chunk exceeds a threshold (600600 ms\nmelody, 800800 ms percussion); without the reset a new chunk would be scheduled\nat a stale future timestamp, producing an audible silent delay.\nBecause the model’s steady-state cadence is ∼2{\\sim}2 s, the reset path\nrather than the crossfade cursor dominates in steady state\n(§6.2).\n\nMultimodal control\n\nFour modalities funnel into the same session\nstate. MIDI: note-on, note-off and CC64 sustain are decoded per input\ndevice and forwarded with note, velocity, event type and timestamp.\nGesture: a MediaPipe Hands (Lugaresi et al., 2019) pipeline\n(maxNumHands=2=2, confidence 0.70.7) infers per-finger extension from\nlandmark ordering and recognizes two robust static poses—index-only toggles\npercussion, open palm toggles the ensemble. Each must be held for 700700 ms\nbefore acting, followed by a 22–33 s cooldown, with hold progress surfaced as\na visual cursor. Voice: continuous Web Speech recognition, gated by\nconfidence ≥0.62\\geq 0.62 and at most eight words, parsed against a rule set\nrequiring an explicit action word paired with a target; a 1.51.5 s deduplication\nwindow prevents double firing. Learn Mode: MusicXML and .mxl are\nparsed entirely in-browser; PDF scores are rendered to page images with PDF.js\nand sent to Gemini vision, which returns a structured note list, with model\nfallbacks tried newest-first.\n\nConstraints that shaped the design\n\nFour properties of the deployed\ngenerator determined non-obvious choices. Lyria cannot change tempo on an open\nstream, so BPM changes are implemented as reconnects and the percussion bed is\ntempo-locked by design. The API exposes no stem-level mixing, so instrument\ncontrol is prompt-level and best-effort. Re-prompting on every note caused\naudible thrashing, motivating the fingerprint-based gate. And the model has no\nnotion of the human’s own sound, so keeping the AI behind the soloist is done\nwith client-side attenuation plus explicit space-enforcing clauses in the prompt.\nThe recurring theme is that fine-grained control over a general model is\nobtained without touching weights: the levers are prompt design,\ngeneration-config scheduling, connection-lifecycle management, and client-side\nmixing.\n\n6 Evaluation\n\nWe evaluate MazzikaAI along three lines: (i) instrumented measurement of\nthe deployed pipeline—latency, throughput, and stream stability\n(§6.1–§6.2); (ii) controlled\nablations that attribute output behaviour to specific design choices over\ninput-identical replayed sessions (§6.4); and (iii) a\nperceptual evaluation in which two expert musicians played the deployed\nsystem (§6.3). The pipeline measurements come from a live\nblues session—latency, gating, and stability are properties of the\ntransport and control loop, not of any genre’s prompt content—while all\noutput-quality experiments target the system’s design centre, Arabic\nmaqam.\n\nApparatus and method\n\nThe setup mirrors the deployment of\n§5: a MIDI keyboard driving the front end in Chromium,\nthe FastAPI backend on the same host, and Lyria RealTime (Google DeepMind, 2025) over\nthe public API. We instrumented every pipeline boundary—key capture, WebSocket\nsend, backend receive, compile completion, prompt push, chunk arrival, client\ndecode, scheduled start—as a timestamped JSONL timeline. Because both processes\nshare a host, client and server wall clocks are directly comparable across the\nboundary; intra-process durations use the monotonic clock. Per-stage figures are\ndifferences between adjacent timestamps. Because a real-time system must be\njudged by its worst cases as well as its typical case, each distribution is\nsummarized by its median together with its 95th and 99th percentiles (p95 and\np99)—the values below which 95%95\\% and 99%99\\% of observations fall,\ncomputed by the nearest-rank method. The end-to-end\nfigure is measured directly rather than summed: when a re-prompt fires, the\nbackend attaches the client wall-clock timestamp of the causal key press to every\nchunk generated under the new prompt, and the client computes the delay from that\npress to the moment the first such chunk is scheduled to become audible. We\nreport a primary session of 8.58.5 min of continuous playing (1,5161{,}516 note\nevents, 758758 onsets, both engines active, 352352 chunks delivered), consistent\nwith a shorter preliminary session whose figures agree within noise.\n\n6.1 Latency and Throughput\n\nFigure 3: Cost of every pipeline stage over the primary 8.58.5 min session: dot\nis the median, the solid bar extends to p95 and the faint bar to p99. The three\nknowledge-based stages (upper band) together cost under a millisecond even at\nthe tail; the generative model and its chunked delivery (lower band) sit three\norders of magnitude higher. Sub-0.10.1 ms values are drawn at the 0.10.1 ms\naxis floor.\n\nFigure 4: (a) Empirical CDF of directly measured key-press-to-audible latency\nagainst the 0.30.3–2.52.5 s answer window the controller itself opens. The\nnote-triggered median of 263263 ms lands at the front of the window and the p99\nof 945945 ms well inside it; the heavier “all re-prompts” tail includes\nheartbeat-fired re-prompts, whose elapsed time from the anchoring key press\nincludes musical waiting rather than pipeline delay. (b) Chunk inter-arrival\ntimes for both engines, concentrated at the model’s 2.02.0 s chunk—generation\nruns at 1×1\\times real time. Gaps ≥5{\\geq}5 s, which span playing pauses and\nstream restarts rather than steady-state delivery, are excluded (44 of 146146\nmelody and 22 of 203203 percussion gaps).\n\nFigures 3–4 and Table 5\nreport the measured decomposition. Three observations follow.\n\nFirst, the knowledge-based layer is essentially free. State update plus\nprompt compilation totals roughly half a millisecond even at p99, and the push\nAPI round trip is sub-millisecond (Fig. 3); the entire budget is\nspent in the generative model and its chunked delivery, which emits uniform\n2.02.0 s chunks at a ∼2.0{\\sim}2.0 s cadence\n(Fig. 4b). This cleanly separates the\ncontribution’s cost from the backend model’s: a faster generator would improve\nresponsiveness without touching the compiler, and the compiler could run\ncomfortably inside a far tighter budget than the one it currently sits in.\n\nSecond, the direct end-to-end median of 263263 ms for note-triggered re-prompts\nsits at the front of the 0.30.3–2.52.5 s answer window encoded in the session\nlogic. This is the sense in which turn-taking buys latency headroom: an\naccompanist’s reply is musically expected to arrive somewhat after the\nsoloist stops, so delay that would be unacceptable for beat-synchronous playing\nis perceptually absorbed by the response gap. Three implementation choices bound\nthe tail deliberately—the bounded queue that drops the oldest chunk rather than\naccumulating backlog, the negative scheduling overlap, and the re-prompt gate\nthat removes per-note compile and API round trips from the hot path. We stress\nthat fitting the reaction budget does not produce beat-level entrainment,\na distinction the expert playing sessions draw sharply (§6.3).\n\nThird, the percussion engine’s much larger push-to-chunk figure\n(median 1,1601{,}160 ms, p99 ≈12{\\approx}12 s) reflects its lifecycle rather than\nits steady state: percussion pushes cluster at style changes and BPM-change\nreconnects, where the stream must be re-established before audio resumes.\n\nTable 5: Measured latency decomposition, primary session (8.58.5 min,\n1,5161{,}516 note events). All values in ms, nearest-rank percentiles. The\n“push→\\tonext chunk” rows report arrival of the first chunk after the new\nprompt—a lower bound on when audibly affected audio arrives, since chunk\ncontent is not attributable to a prompt without audio analysis. The\nchunk-cadence rows are inter-arrival gaps between an engine’s consecutive\nserver-side emission records (147147 melody, 204204 percussion;\nTable 6), excluding the six gaps ≥5{\\geq}5 s that span\nplaying pauses and stream restarts rather than steady-state delivery (44\nmelody, 22 percussion), hence n=142n{=}142 of 146146 and n=201n{=}201 of 203203\ngaps. The percussion engine is soloist-decoupled by design and so has no\nend-to-end row.\n\nStage\nmedian\np95\np99\nnn\n\nKnowledge-based layer\n\nBackend state update\n0.30.3\n0.50.5\n0.60.6\n3,5553{,}555\n\nPrompt compile\n0.20.2\n0.30.3\n0.40.4\n3,5553{,}555\n\nRe-prompt push (API round trip)\n0.30.3\n0.80.8\n0.90.9\n1,4011{,}401\n\nTransport and client\n\nKey capture →\\rightarrow WS send\n\n<1<\\!1\n11\n22\n1,5161{,}516\n\nWS transport up (note events)\n1.31.3\n8.78.7\n24.224.2\n1,5161{,}516\n\nWS transport down (chunks)\n33.933.9\n40.840.8\n44.144.1\n352352\n\nClient decode ++ schedule\n\n3.13.1\n5.55.5\n9.09.0\n352352\n\nGenerative model\n\nPush →\\rightarrow next chunk (melody)\n\n150150\n1,0701{,}070\n1,7991{,}799\n140140\n\nPush →\\rightarrow next chunk (perc.)\n\n1,1601{,}160\n3,2503{,}250\n11,93411{,}934\n3030\n\nChunk cadence (melody)\n1,9991{,}999\n2,9782{,}978\n3,4593{,}459\n142142\n\nChunk cadence (percussion)\n2,0242{,}024\n2,9812{,}981\n3,4823{,}482\n201201\n\nEnd-to-end (directly measured)\n\nNote-triggered re-prompts\n263263\n818818\n945945\n9898\n\nAll re-prompts\n358358\n2,3342{,}334\n5,3555{,}355\n140140\n\n6.2 Gating and Stream Stability\n\nFigure 5: (a) Gate decisions by driver, split into re-prompts fired and decisions\nabsorbed, with the absorbed percentage above each bar: the gate absorbs 74%74\\%\nof heartbeat-driven decisions and 15%15\\% of note-driven ones. (b)\nRe-steering rate against note-onset rate in 1010 s bins: re-prompts track\nplaying activity closely and average 179179 per minute, with zero queue drops,\nreconnects, or stream failures across the session.\n\nThe central result of this subsection is one of stability: the\npipeline tolerates an order of magnitude more re-steering than its design\nassumed. Over the primary session, 1,5171{,}517 gate fires (179179 per\nminute—roughly one re-steer per generated 22 s chunk) reached the engines\nas 1,4011{,}401 API pushes (166166 per minute), and the stream exhibited zero\nfailures: no reconnects, no stream terminations, and no bounded-queue drops,\nwith delivery continuing at the model’s native cadence throughout\n(Fig. 5b, Table 6). The practical ceiling on\nre-prompt frequency is therefore set by musical coherence—how often the\nmodel should be re-steered—rather than by transport or model\nstability. We scope the claim precisely: two service-side disconnects\n(1011 service unavailable) occurred in the same log, at 631631 s and\n684684 s—after playing had stopped, during an idle tail in which the\nbrowser was left connected. Both were handled by the engine’s self-healing\npath; they bear on idle-session lifetime, not on stability under load.\n\nThe gate itself absorbed 43.1%43.1\\% of the 2,6652{,}665 decisions it evaluated:\n74.4%74.4\\% of heartbeat-driven decisions and 15.1%15.1\\% of note-driven ones\n(Fig. 5a). The lower note-driven selectivity traces to the\nharmony fingerprint, whose 22-semitone pitch-class buckets over a 22 s\nwindow are fine enough that most new melodic pitches change the bucket set\nduring active playing. The fingerprint’s granularity is a tunable parameter,\nand coarsening it—or adding hysteresis—is a directly measurable\nrefinement (§7.4). On the client side, chunk-boundary\ncontinuity in steady state rests chiefly on the regularity of chunk arrival\n(Fig. 4b); the crossfade cursor of\n§5 engages mainly during burst deliveries, and\nre-anchoring its reset condition to the scheduled playback horizon rather\nthan the arrival gap is a mechanical refinement.\n\nTable 6: Measured gating and streaming-stability summary, primary session.\nFailure counters are for the 8.58.5 min active window; the two service-side\ndisconnects in the same log occurred later, in the idle tail\n(§6.2). The per-engine chunk split is taken from client-side\ndelivery records; the server-side emission log captured 351351 of the 352352\ndeliveries (147147 melody, 204204 percussion), one melody log write having been\ndropped at a stream boundary.\n\nQuantity\n\nValue\n\nSession length / note events / onsets\n\n8.58.5 min / 1,5161{,}516 / 758758\n\nGate decisions evaluated\n\n2,6652{,}665\n\n  absorbed, all drivers\n\n1,1481{,}148 (43.1%43.1\\%)\n", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1100, "totalLines": 2437}}}
