{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-986c18034d86eafd", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1600\t  absorbed, note-driven only\n1601\t\n1602\t212212 of 1,4021{,}402 (15.1%15.1\\%)\n1603\t\n1604\t  absorbed, heartbeat-driven\n1605\t\n1606\t936936 of 1,2581{,}258 (74.4%74.4\\%)\n1607\t\n1608\tGate fires / API pushes\n1609\t\n1610\t1,5171{,}517 / 1,4011{,}401\n1611\t\n1612\tRe-prompt rate (fires; pushes)\n1613\t\n1614\t179179; 166166 min-1\n1615\t\n1616\tAudio chunks delivered (melody; perc.)\n1617\t\n1618\t352352 (148148; 204204)\n1619\t\n1620\tBounded-queue drops\n1621\t\n1622\t0\n1623\t\n1624\tLyria reconnects; stream terminations\n1625\t\n1626\t0; 0\n1627\t\n1628\t6.3 Perceptual Evaluation: Expert Playing Sessions\n1629\t\n1630\tBecause several of the system’s target properties—coherent dialogue,\n1631\tidiomatic ensemble character, timing fit—are ultimately perceptual, we\n1632\tcomplemented the instrumented measurements with playing sessions in which two\n1633\texpert musicians independently played the deployed system and provided\n1634\tqualitative feedback. We characterize the scope precisely: these sessions\n1635\tconstitute a small-scale perceptual evaluation by domain experts interacting\n1636\twith the live system, not a controlled listening study with a larger\n1637\tparticipant pool; the latter remains future work (§7.4).\n1638\t\n1639\tThe most consistent feedback concerned temporal responsiveness. The\n1640\tperformers perceived the accompaniment as holding an essentially constant\n1641\ttempo rather than breathing with their playing—the ensemble kept its own\n1642\tclock. This observation is consistent with the system’s construction. The\n1643\tgenerator’s tempo is fixed for the lifetime of a stream; the percussion bed\n1644\tis tempo-locked by design; and the melodic engine’s “following” is\n1645\tsemantic rather than metric—the compiler tracks register,\n1646\tdensity, dynamics, harmony, and phrase boundaries, but performs no beat\n1647\ttracking on the performer’s onsets, so nothing in the control loop re-anchors\n1648\tgeneration to the actual pulse. The compiler currently controls what\n1649\tthe accompaniment plays more precisely than when.\n1650\t\n1651\tThis finding has three consequences. It identifies tempo and timing\n1652\tfit as a rated construct—distinct from the reaction-latency sense of\n1653\tresponsiveness measured in §6.1—for the planned\n1654\tlarger-scale study. It sharpens the latency argument: turn-taking headroom\n1655\tabsorbs reaction delay but does not substitute for beat-level entrainment.\n1656\tAnd it places beat tracking with tempo re-anchoring at musically safe\n1657\tboundaries at the head of the future-work agenda.\n1658\t\n1659\t6.4 Objective Ablation Results\n1660\t\n1661\tThree ablations are measurable without listeners, and we report them here as\n1662\texecuted experiments. The remaining, inherently perceptual comparisons are\n1663\tdeferred to the planned controlled study (§7.4).\n1664\t\n1665\tMethod: input-identical replay\n1666\t\n1667\tAll conditions are driven by a\n1668\treplay harness that feeds the recorded input stream of the primary\n1669\tsession—every note event at its original pacing—through the deployed\n1670\tbackend over the same WebSocket path a live client uses. Conditions therefore\n1671\treceive byte-identical performer input and differ only in a single\n1672\tenvironment-selected flag: condition B replaces the maqam grounding clause\n1673\twith a generic Western-scale phrase of matched length; condition C drops the\n1674\tleading instrument-constraint (silence) clause; the gating arms set the\n1675\tre-prompt gate to on, off (fire on every evaluation), or\n1676\tstatic (compile once, never re-steer). Every session log records its\n1677\town condition flags in its first record, so each run is self-describing, and\n1678\tevery generated audio chunk is written to disk tagged with the prompt\n1679\tsequence number under which it was generated. Because the generator is a live\n1680\tstochastic service, identical inputs do not produce identical audio; we\n1681\ttherefore replay multiple arms per condition and report distributions.\n1682\tReplay scripts, per-arm logs, and analysis code are released with the paper.\n1683\t\n1684\tMaqam grounding (A vs. B)\n1685\t\n1686\tThree arms per condition\n1687\t(553553 vs. 521521 generated melody chunks; ∼18{\\sim}18 min of audio each)\n1688\tunder the bayati configuration, analyzed two ways: a polyphony-safe\n1689\tconstant-QQ energy profile at 1010-cent resolution, and pYIN predominant-F0F_{0}\n1690\tframe statistics (Fig. 6). The grounding clause\n1691\tdemonstrably shapes pitch content, though not deterministically: with\n1692\tgrounding, the tonic D is the most energetic pitch class pooled (share\n1693\t0.1400.140) and in two of three arms (0.1750.175, 0.1630.163), with one arm failing\n1694\tto anchor (0.0800.080, ranked sixth); without grounding D never ranks first in\n1695\tany arm (pooled 0.0940.094, ranked fourth). The per-arm spread is itself a\n1696\tfinding about steering a stochastic hosted generator: grounding shifts the\n1697\tdistribution of renderings rather than guaranteeing any single one. By frame statistics, 22.4%22.4\\% of grounded voiced frames lie\n1698\t≥35{\\geq}35 cents off the 12-TET grid versus 14.9%14.9\\% ablated\n1699\t(95%95\\% CIs [21.7,23.2][21.7,23.2] vs. [14.0,15.7][14.0,15.7]), and—most directly—when\n1700\tthe melodic line visits the second-degree region above D, 59.4%59.4\\% of\n1701\tgrounded frames fall in the half-flat band (125125–175175 cents, E koron)\n1702\tagainst 24.1%24.1\\% ablated (n=650n{=}650 vs. 1,5301{,}530 frames). At the same time\n1703\tthe aggregate energy profile shows no concentrated 150150-cent peak:\n1704\tsecond-degree energy splits nearly evenly across E♭\\flat/E-half-flat/E\n1705\tin both conditions, a result robust to harmonic–percussive separation. In\n1706\tsummary, prompt-level grounding elicits genuinely microtonal melodic\n1707\tinflection—the lead line spends significantly more time at quarter-tone\n1708\tpitch—while rendering the half-flat second as a stably tuned scale degree\n1709\tremains beyond prompt-level control alone.\n1710\t\n1711\tFigure 6: Maqam-grounding ablation, three input-identical arms per condition\n1712\tpooled. (Left) Constant-QQ energy versus deviation from the nearest 12-TET\n1713\tsemitone. (Right) Pitch-class energy in cents above the tonic D; the shaded\n1714\tband marks the half-flat second degree (125125–175175 cents) that maqam bayati\n1715\tprescribes. Aggregate energy shows no concentrated quarter-tone peak, while\n1716\tframe-level statistics (text) show the grounded lead line visiting the\n1717\thalf-flat band significantly more often.\n1718\t\n1719\tInstrument suppression (A vs. C)\n1720\t\n1721\tAn audit with the PANNs Cnn14\n1722\tAudioSet tagger (clip-probability threshold 0.10.1, 95%95\\% Wilson intervals,\n1723\tsame-day arms to control for service drift) finds that the leading\n1724\tinstrument-constraint clause does not measurably suppress the excluded\n1725\tsection instruments; detections of the excluded cello class were, if\n1726\tanything, more frequent with the clause present, consistent with the known\n1727\ttendency of negative prompting to evoke what it names. Absolute rates carry\n1728\ta caveat—AudioSet has no oud class, so plucked-string content may register\n1729\tas cello in both conditions. Prompt-level prohibition is therefore not a\n1730\tsubstitute for stem-level API control, and we bound the corresponding claim\n1731\taccordingly (§7.3).\n1732\t\n1733\tGating ablation (on / off / static)\n1734\t\n1735\tInput-identical replays under\n1736\tthe three gate modes (Table 7). Disabling the gate raises\n1737\tthe push rate from 173173 to 202202 pushes/min—every gate evaluation becomes\n1738\tan API push—yet the stream again exhibits zero failures: no queue\n1739\tdrops, no reconnects, no terminations, and an unchanged 2.02.0 s delivery\n1740\tcadence. This replicates, under deliberately adversarial re-steering, the\n1741\tstability result of §6.2: the gate’s measurable\n1742\tcontribution is API economy (43.9%43.9\\% of decisions absorbed; 16%16\\% fewer\n1743\tpushes), not stream protection. The static arm isolates the compiler’s\n1744\tvalue: audio continues to arrive at the model’s native cadence, but\n1745\ttrigger-to-chunk latency collapses from a 470470 ms median to\n1746\t∼35{\\sim}35 s—the stream simply stops tracking the performer. The replayed\n1747\tgate-on arm also reproduces the live session’s gating profile (43.9%43.9\\%\n1748\tvs. 43.1%43.1\\% absorbed), validating the replay methodology itself.\n1749\t\n1750\tTable 7: Gating ablation over input-identical replays of the primary\n1751\tsession. “e2e” is trigger→\\tochunk-received, reconstructed by the replay\n1752\tclient (∼3{\\sim}3–99 ms tighter than the live browser measurement); the\n1753\tstatic arm’s e2e reflects chunks no longer being attributable to recent\n1754\tinput rather than pipeline delay.\n1755\t\n1756\ton\n1757\toff\n1758\tstatic\n1759\t\n1760\tGate decisions\n1761\t2,8702{,}870\n1762\t2,9462{,}946\n1763\t2,9462{,}946\n1764\t\n1765\tfired\n1766\t1,6111{,}611\n1767\t2,9462{,}946\n1768\t1010\n1769\t\n1770\tabsorbed\n1771\t43.9%43.9\\%\n1772\t0%0\\%\n1773\t99.7%99.7\\%\n1774\t\n1775\tAPI pushes (min-1)\n1776\t\n1777\t173173\n1778\t202202\n1779\t1.11.1\n1780\t\n1781\tQueue drops / reconnects / stream ends\n1782\t0\n1783\t0\n1784\t0\n1785\t\n1786\tChunk cadence median (ms)\n1787\t2,0062{,}006\n1788\t1,9991{,}999\n1789\t2,0052{,}005\n1790\t\n1791\te2e median (ms)\n1792\t470470\n1793\t457457\n1794\t34,82834{,}828\n1795\t\n1796\t7 Discussion\n1797\t\n1798\t7.1 Why Prompt Compilation Works\n1799\t\n1800\tInterposing a natural-language prompt between the estimated performance state and\n1801\tthe generator—rather than exposing the model to raw MIDI, learned latents, or a\n1802\tbespoke control API—buys four properties at once.\n1803\t\n1804\tIt is a model-agnostic surface: every current text-conditioned music\n1805\tmodel (Google DeepMind, 2025; Copet et al., 2023; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) accepts free-form prose,\n1806\tso compiling state into prose rather than into one model’s private\n1807\tparameterization treats the generator as an interchangeable backend, and\n1808\tretargeting is a matter of re-tuning phrasing and the parameter table rather than\n1809\tre-architecting the pipeline. It requires no training: no paired\n1810\tsoloist/accompaniment corpus, no gradient step, no adapter—all the musical\n1811\tknowledge the system contributes (Table 4) is deterministic,\n1812\thuman-authored prose, which lets a small team encode substantial expertise\n1813\twithout data-collection or training infrastructure. It is inspectable and\n1814\teditable clause by clause: every accompaniment decision is traceable to a\n1815\tspecific branch of the compiler, a musician can read the exact string sent to the\n1816\tmodel and edit the phrasing to change the behaviour, and—as the stray\n1817\tscale: bayati clause in Listing 3\n1818\tshows—compiler defects surface as legible English rather than as a silent shift\n1819\tin a latent. And it is deterministic, which is what makes gating\n1820\tsound at all: because the compiler is a pure function of state, the system can\n1821\tcheaply detect whether a re-prompt would be musically meaningful before paying\n1822\tfor it.\n1823\t\n1824\tThe measurements add a fifth, quantitative property. The knowledge-based layer\n1825\tcosts under a millisecond end to end (Fig. 3) against a\n1826\tgenerator budget three decades larger. Symbolic control of a streaming\n1827\tfoundation model is therefore not a performance compromise: on this pipeline the\n1828\texpert system is free, and the design question is entirely about what the prose\n1829\tshould say and how often it should change.\n1830\t\n1831\tIn this respect MazzikaAI stands in the lineage of knowledge-based systems for\n1832\tmusic such as CHORAL (Ebcioğlu, 1988)—expertise captured in an\n1833\texplicit, human-readable representation and applied by deterministic rules—with\n1834\tthe effector replaced. That substitution is the transferable idea. The\n1835\tarchitecture it embodies (estimate live human state →\\to compile to prose →\\to\n1836\tsteer a streaming generator →\\to render →\\to loop) is not specific to music: the\n1837\tsame loop could plausibly drive live visuals from pose estimation, steer a text\n1838\tgenerator from a running model of a document and its recent edits, or extend code\n1839\tfrom the evolving structure of a program. In each case the load-bearing\n1840\tengineering is identical to ours—a deterministic, domain-informed compiler plus\n1841\ta policy for when to re-steer. We do not claim to have validated these transfers;\n1842\twe note only that the pattern appears reusable.\n1843\t\n1844\t7.2 Cultural and Microtonal Fidelity\n1845\t\n1846\tPrompt-level grounding is a lightweight and inspectable path toward non-Western\n1847\tidioms (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021; Bozkurt et al., 2014): it needs no specialized model and no\n1848\tretraining, and the entire cultural encoding is prose that a maqam expert can\n1849\tread, critique, and correct. But a lightweight instructional path is not faithful\n1850\trendering, and the distinction must be drawn sharply. The prompt can\n1851\tinstruct the model to shimmer on a half-flat E and to slide\n1852\tD→E​-half-flatD\\!\\rightarrow\\!E\\text{-half-flat}; whether the model renders that degree\n1853\tat its true pitch is a separate question governed by its training data. General\n1854\ttext-to-music models are trained predominantly on twelve-tone equal-tempered\n1855\tWestern material (Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023), and their\n1856\tinternal representation of pitch may under-represent quarter-tone intervals\n1857\tentirely. The pitch analysis of §6.4 now gives this\n1858\tquestion a measured, partial answer. Grounding does not merely decorate: it\n1859\tanchors generation on the prescribed tonic and moves a significantly larger\n1860\tshare of the melodic line off the equal-tempered grid, with the second degree\n1861\tvisited predominantly in the half-flat band. But the aggregate tuning\n1862\tevidence is equally clear that the half-flat second is not rendered as a\n1863\tstable scale degree—the model inflects toward the quarter-tone\n1864\trather than tuning to it. Whether that inflection-without-anchoring profile\n1865\tis heard by maqam-trained musicians as expressive authenticity or as\n1866\tout-of-tune approximation is a perceptual question that pitch measurement\n1867\talone cannot settle; it is a central hypothesis for the controlled listening\n1868\tstudy planned as future work (§7.4).\n1869\t\n1870\t7.3 Limitations\n1871\t\n1872\tWe describe MazzikaAI as a working system and a methodological contribution, not\n1873\tan empirically validated result. Five limitations bound the claims.\n1874\t\n1875\tNo large-scale user study. We report instrumented system\n1876\tmeasurements, objective output-quality ablations, and a qualitative\n1877\tperceptual evaluation with two expert musicians (§6.3);\n1878\ta controlled listening study with a larger participant pool remains to be\n1879\trun. The live measurement session was performed by the first author, and the\n1880\tablation arms replay that same performance, so behavioural coverage across\n1881\tplayers and genres remains to be established.\n1882\t\n1883\tConstraint language is not honoured. Because the API exposes no\n1884\tstem-level control, unwanted instruments are addressed purely through negative\n1885\tprompt language—and the audit of §6.4 shows this\n1886\tmechanism failing: excluded-instrument detections were more frequent\n1887\twith the silence clause than without it. More broadly the whole\n1888\tcontrol scheme assumes the generator obeys constraint-style instructions\n1889\t(avoid these tonalities, exclude vocals, hold this tempo), and when it does\n1890\tnot, the compiler has no lower-level fallback; per-stem API control, where it\n1891\tbecomes available, is the principled fix.\n1892\t\n1893\tDependence on a proprietary hosted model, and a latency floor set by it.\n1894\tLyria RealTime is closed and subject to availability, quota and behavioural\n1895\tchange outside our control; its inability to re-tempo a live stream shapes the\n1896\tarchitecture, and its 22 s chunk granularity sets the floor beneath the\n1897\tmeasured 263263 ms median. Two service-side disconnects in our own log\n1898\t(§6.2) illustrate the exposure.\n1899\t\n1900\tNarrow, hand-authored coverage in places. Harmonic inference is rule-based\n1901\tand partly blues-specific—the implied-chord logic infers a twelve-bar A7/D7/E7\n1902\tregion from a pitch-class histogram and does not generalize to arbitrary keys\n1903\twithout further rules. The state estimator models one player on one keyboard and\n1904\thas no notion of multiple simultaneous performers. Voice commands are\n1905\tEnglish-only, a notable gap given the system’s Arabic-music focus. And gesture\n1906\tcontrol depends on lighting, camera placement and a hold-plus-cooldown protocol,\n1907\tand has not been evaluated under stage conditions.\n1908\t\n1909\t7.4 Future Work\n1910\t\n1911\tFour directions follow directly from the measurements. Beat tracking and\n1912\ttempo following is the priority: estimate the performer’s tempo and beat phase\n1913\tfrom the onset stream—the estimator already computes notes-per-second and\n1914\tinter-onset gaps—and re-anchor both engines to it, absorbing the reconnect cost\n1915\tat musically safe boundaries such as phrase ends and long idles.\n1916\tRecalibrating the gate is now a measurable exercise rather than a guess:\n1917\tcoarsen the harmony fingerprint or add hysteresis, and sweep re-prompt frequency\n1918\tagainst perceived coherence—the gating ablation establishes that stability is\n1919\tnot the binding constraint, so the sweep is bounded only by musical coherence.\n1920\tPerceptual validation means scaling the expert playing sessions of\n1921\t§6.3 into a controlled listening study, whose hypotheses\n1922\tthe executed ablations have sharpened;\n1923\tthe replay harness additionally enables objective MIR measures not yet\n1924\tcomputed, such as harmonic agreement with the soloist and rhythmic alignment.\n1925\tAnd reducing platform dependence means\n1926\tretargeting the compiler to an open-weights streaming\n1927\tmodel (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) for on-device operation, which would remove both the\n1928\tnetwork latency floor and the proprietary-API exposure. Further out, a hybrid\n1929\tcompiler could tune phrasing or timing from performance data while retaining the\n1930\tinspectable text interface, the estimator could track several simultaneous\n1931\tsoloists, and per-stem control—if future APIs expose it—would replace\n1932\tbest-effort prompt suppression with guaranteed mixing.\n1933\t\n1934\t8 Conclusion\n1935\t\n1936\tWe presented MazzikaAI, a real-time accompaniment system built around one\n1937\tmethodological idea: a deterministic, musically-informed compiler from live\n1938\tperformance state to natural-language prompts. Architecturally it is a\n1939\tknowledge-based system in the classical sense—an inspectable hand-authored\n1940\tknowledge base, a working memory holding the estimated performance state, and a\n1941\trule-based four-state inference layer—whose actuator is prose consumed by a\n1942\tpretrained streaming generator rather than a symbolic effector. That substitution\n1943\tis what lets the system obtain idiomatic, microtonal call-and-response\n1944\taccompaniment for Arabic maqam performance with no fine-tuning of any\n1945\tkind—and to retarget to eight further Western genres at the cost of\n1946\tauthoring prompt fragments.\n1947\t\n1948\tInstrumenting the deployed pipeline sharpens the claim in both directions. The\n1949\tknowledge-based layer costs under a millisecond against a generator budget three\n1950\tdecades larger, so symbolic control of a streaming foundation model is\n1951\tessentially free; the median key-to-audible-update latency of 263263 ms lands at\n1952\tthe front of the answer window the controller itself opens; and the stream\n1953\ttolerates 179179 re-prompts per minute without a single failure—and, in the\n1954\tgating ablation, 202202 pushes per minute with the gate disabled entirely.\n1955\tControlled ablations over input-identical replayed sessions attribute\n1956\tbehaviour to design choices: maqam grounding shifts generation toward the\n1957\ttonic and yields significantly more quarter-tone melodic content; a static\n1958\tprompt lets trigger-to-audio latency collapse to tens of seconds, quantifying\n1959\twhat the compiler buys. The same instrumentation also marks the current\n1960\tlimits of prompt-level control—most notably, constraint-style prohibition\n1961\tis no substitute for stem-level mixing. A perceptual evaluation with two\n1962\texpert musicians localizes the remaining gap precisely: the compiler controls\n1963\twhat the accompaniment plays far more accurately than when it\n1964\tplays it.\n1965\t\n1966\tThe broader significance is that much of the intelligence a responsive creative\n1967\tpartner needs can be located not in model weights but in a transparent,\n1968\tknowledge-based compiler that translates live human state into language a general\n1969\tmodel already understands—and that on current hardware this layer is cheap\n1970\tenough to be free. As streaming generative models improve, we expect the value of\n1971\tsuch lightweight, inspectable, idiom-aware control layers to grow.\n1972\t\n1973\tCRediT authorship contribution statement\n1974\t\n1975\tJiaxin Du: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Investigation,\n1976\tWriting – original draft, Writing – review & editing.\n1977\tYong Zhuang: Validation, Investigation, Writing – review & editing.\n1978\tHaoyu Li: Investigation, Writing – review & editing.\n1979\t\n1980\tDeclaration of competing interest\n1981\t\n1982\tThe authors declare no known competing financial interests or personal\n1983\trelationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this\n1984\tpaper.\n1985\t\n1986\tData availability\n1987\t\n1988\tThe replication package for this article is deposited on figshare\n1989\t(DOI: 10.6084/m9.figshare.XXXXXXX). The deposit contains\n1990\tthe system source code; the primary-session instrumentation log underlying\n1991\tFigs. 3–5 and\n1992\tTables 5–6; the replay harness, condition\n1993\tflags, and per-arm session logs for every ablation arm of\n1994\t§6.4 (each log records its own condition in its first\n1995\trecord); a run manifest mapping arms to logs; the analysis scripts and\n1996\ttheir JSON outputs behind Fig. 6 and\n1997\tTable 7; and the generated audio chunks\n1998\t(∼420{\\sim}420 MB) for every reported experiment arm. Replaying an arm\n1999\treproduces the input exactly; because the deployed generative model (Google\n2000\tLyria RealTime) is a hosted stochastic service accessed through its public\n2001\tAPI, regenerated audio varies across runs, and the deposited audio and\n2002\tanalysis outputs document the arms reported here.\n2003\t\n2004\tAppendix A Genre Catalogue\n2005\t\n2006\tBeyond its Arabic-maqam core, the compiler carries eight further Western\n2007\tgenres (Table 8). They exist chiefly as evidence for the\n2008\tadaptation-cost claim of §2: each was added by authoring a\n2009\tgenre configuration—section list and order, per-role prompt language, a\n2010\tlead-preference list, header text, generation-parameter rows, and percussion\n2011\tpatterns—with no change to the estimator, controller, or transport. Arabic\n2012\tand blues use bespoke controllers with idiom-specific harmonic and echo\n2013\tlogic; the remaining genres share a generic controller driven entirely by\n2014\tthe configuration record. Output quality outside the Arabic design centre is\n2015\tnot evaluated in this paper and is expected to be weaker, in proportion to\n2016\tthe depth of each genre’s authored knowledge.\n2017\t\n2018\tTable 8: The nine genres: instrument sections (low→\\rightarrowhigh), lead\n2019\tinstrument, and controller.\n2020\t\n2021\tGenre\n2022\tSections (low→\\rightarrowhigh)\n2023\tLead\n2024\tController\n2025\t\n2026\tvintage arabic\n2027\tcello, oud, strings, qanun, nay\n2028\tnay\n2029\tbespoke (takht)\n2030\t\n2031\tblues\n2032\tbass, rhythm gtr, piano, harmonica, lead gtr\n2033\tharmonica\n2034\tbespoke\n2035\t\n2036\tjazz fusion\n2037\tupright bass, piano, jazz gtr, sax, trumpet\n2038\tsaxophone\n2039\tgeneric\n2040\t\n2041\trock & roll\n2042\tbass, rhythm gtr, piano/keys, lead gtr\n2043\tlead guitar\n2044\tgeneric\n2045\t\n2046\tclassical\n2047\tcello, viola, violin, oboe\n2048\toboe\n2049\tgeneric\n2050\t\n2051\tmodern pop\n2052\tbass synth, pad synth, keys, vocal aah\n2053\tvocal aah\n2054\tgeneric\n2055\t\n2056\tflamenco\n2057\tcello, violin, flamenco gtr\n2058\tflamenco gtr\n2059\tgeneric\n2060\t\n2061\tcinematic\n2062\tcello, strings, brass pad, piano/harp\n2063\tpiano\n2064\tgeneric\n2065\t\n2066\tfunk\n2067\tbass, rhythm gtr, keys/organ, horns\n2068\thorn section\n2069\tgeneric\n2070\t\n2071\tReferences\n2072\t\n2073\tA. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank (2023)\n2074\tMusicLM: generating music from text.\n2075\t\n2076\tarXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325.\n2077\t\n2078\tCited by: §1,\n2079\t§2,\n2080\tTable 1,\n2081\t§7.2.\n2082\t\n2083\tZ. Borsos, R. Marinier, D. Vincent, E. Kharitonov, O. Pietquin, M. Sharifi, D. Roblek, O. Teboul, D. Grangier, M. Tagliasacchi, and N. Zeghidour (2023)\n2084\tAudioLM: a language modeling approach to audio generation.\n2085\t\n2086\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 31, pp. 2523–2533.\n2087\t\n2088\tExternal Links: Document\n2089\t\n2090\tCited by: §2.\n2091\t\n2092\tB. Bozkurt, R. Ayangil, and A. Holzapfel (2014)\n2093\tComputational analysis of Turkish makam music: review of state-of-the-art and challenges.\n2094\t\n2095\tJournal of New Music Research 43 (1), pp. 3–23.\n2096\t\n2097\tExternal Links: Document\n2098\t\n2099\tCited by: §1,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "810faa67-2c27-451c-a35b-94f5fe6397c3", "uuid": "c3d70e73-c0fc-496a-be5e-d248fb168619", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T06:34:36.587Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/mazzika_text.txt", "content": "  absorbed, note-driven only\n\n212212 of 1,4021{,}402 (15.1%15.1\\%)\n\n  absorbed, heartbeat-driven\n\n936936 of 1,2581{,}258 (74.4%74.4\\%)\n\nGate fires / API pushes\n\n1,5171{,}517 / 1,4011{,}401\n\nRe-prompt rate (fires; pushes)\n\n179179; 166166 min-1\n\nAudio chunks delivered (melody; perc.)\n\n352352 (148148; 204204)\n\nBounded-queue drops\n\n0\n\nLyria reconnects; stream terminations\n\n0; 0\n\n6.3 Perceptual Evaluation: Expert Playing Sessions\n\nBecause several of the system’s target properties—coherent dialogue,\nidiomatic ensemble character, timing fit—are ultimately perceptual, we\ncomplemented the instrumented measurements with playing sessions in which two\nexpert musicians independently played the deployed system and provided\nqualitative feedback. We characterize the scope precisely: these sessions\nconstitute a small-scale perceptual evaluation by domain experts interacting\nwith the live system, not a controlled listening study with a larger\nparticipant pool; the latter remains future work (§7.4).\n\nThe most consistent feedback concerned temporal responsiveness. The\nperformers perceived the accompaniment as holding an essentially constant\ntempo rather than breathing with their playing—the ensemble kept its own\nclock. This observation is consistent with the system’s construction. The\ngenerator’s tempo is fixed for the lifetime of a stream; the percussion bed\nis tempo-locked by design; and the melodic engine’s “following” is\nsemantic rather than metric—the compiler tracks register,\ndensity, dynamics, harmony, and phrase boundaries, but performs no beat\ntracking on the performer’s onsets, so nothing in the control loop re-anchors\ngeneration to the actual pulse. The compiler currently controls what\nthe accompaniment plays more precisely than when.\n\nThis finding has three consequences. It identifies tempo and timing\nfit as a rated construct—distinct from the reaction-latency sense of\nresponsiveness measured in §6.1—for the planned\nlarger-scale study. It sharpens the latency argument: turn-taking headroom\nabsorbs reaction delay but does not substitute for beat-level entrainment.\nAnd it places beat tracking with tempo re-anchoring at musically safe\nboundaries at the head of the future-work agenda.\n\n6.4 Objective Ablation Results\n\nThree ablations are measurable without listeners, and we report them here as\nexecuted experiments. The remaining, inherently perceptual comparisons are\ndeferred to the planned controlled study (§7.4).\n\nMethod: input-identical replay\n\nAll conditions are driven by a\nreplay harness that feeds the recorded input stream of the primary\nsession—every note event at its original pacing—through the deployed\nbackend over the same WebSocket path a live client uses. Conditions therefore\nreceive byte-identical performer input and differ only in a single\nenvironment-selected flag: condition B replaces the maqam grounding clause\nwith a generic Western-scale phrase of matched length; condition C drops the\nleading instrument-constraint (silence) clause; the gating arms set the\nre-prompt gate to on, off (fire on every evaluation), or\nstatic (compile once, never re-steer). Every session log records its\nown condition flags in its first record, so each run is self-describing, and\nevery generated audio chunk is written to disk tagged with the prompt\nsequence number under which it was generated. Because the generator is a live\nstochastic service, identical inputs do not produce identical audio; we\ntherefore replay multiple arms per condition and report distributions.\nReplay scripts, per-arm logs, and analysis code are released with the paper.\n\nMaqam grounding (A vs. B)\n\nThree arms per condition\n(553553 vs. 521521 generated melody chunks; ∼18{\\sim}18 min of audio each)\nunder the bayati configuration, analyzed two ways: a polyphony-safe\nconstant-QQ energy profile at 1010-cent resolution, and pYIN predominant-F0F_{0}\nframe statistics (Fig. 6). The grounding clause\ndemonstrably shapes pitch content, though not deterministically: with\ngrounding, the tonic D is the most energetic pitch class pooled (share\n0.1400.140) and in two of three arms (0.1750.175, 0.1630.163), with one arm failing\nto anchor (0.0800.080, ranked sixth); without grounding D never ranks first in\nany arm (pooled 0.0940.094, ranked fourth). The per-arm spread is itself a\nfinding about steering a stochastic hosted generator: grounding shifts the\ndistribution of renderings rather than guaranteeing any single one. By frame statistics, 22.4%22.4\\% of grounded voiced frames lie\n≥35{\\geq}35 cents off the 12-TET grid versus 14.9%14.9\\% ablated\n(95%95\\% CIs [21.7,23.2][21.7,23.2] vs. [14.0,15.7][14.0,15.7]), and—most directly—when\nthe melodic line visits the second-degree region above D, 59.4%59.4\\% of\ngrounded frames fall in the half-flat band (125125–175175 cents, E koron)\nagainst 24.1%24.1\\% ablated (n=650n{=}650 vs. 1,5301{,}530 frames). At the same time\nthe aggregate energy profile shows no concentrated 150150-cent peak:\nsecond-degree energy splits nearly evenly across E♭\\flat/E-half-flat/E\nin both conditions, a result robust to harmonic–percussive separation. In\nsummary, prompt-level grounding elicits genuinely microtonal melodic\ninflection—the lead line spends significantly more time at quarter-tone\npitch—while rendering the half-flat second as a stably tuned scale degree\nremains beyond prompt-level control alone.\n\nFigure 6: Maqam-grounding ablation, three input-identical arms per condition\npooled. (Left) Constant-QQ energy versus deviation from the nearest 12-TET\nsemitone. (Right) Pitch-class energy in cents above the tonic D; the shaded\nband marks the half-flat second degree (125125–175175 cents) that maqam bayati\nprescribes. Aggregate energy shows no concentrated quarter-tone peak, while\nframe-level statistics (text) show the grounded lead line visiting the\nhalf-flat band significantly more often.\n\nInstrument suppression (A vs. C)\n\nAn audit with the PANNs Cnn14\nAudioSet tagger (clip-probability threshold 0.10.1, 95%95\\% Wilson intervals,\nsame-day arms to control for service drift) finds that the leading\ninstrument-constraint clause does not measurably suppress the excluded\nsection instruments; detections of the excluded cello class were, if\nanything, more frequent with the clause present, consistent with the known\ntendency of negative prompting to evoke what it names. Absolute rates carry\na caveat—AudioSet has no oud class, so plucked-string content may register\nas cello in both conditions. Prompt-level prohibition is therefore not a\nsubstitute for stem-level API control, and we bound the corresponding claim\naccordingly (§7.3).\n\nGating ablation (on / off / static)\n\nInput-identical replays under\nthe three gate modes (Table 7). Disabling the gate raises\nthe push rate from 173173 to 202202 pushes/min—every gate evaluation becomes\nan API push—yet the stream again exhibits zero failures: no queue\ndrops, no reconnects, no terminations, and an unchanged 2.02.0 s delivery\ncadence. This replicates, under deliberately adversarial re-steering, the\nstability result of §6.2: the gate’s measurable\ncontribution is API economy (43.9%43.9\\% of decisions absorbed; 16%16\\% fewer\npushes), not stream protection. The static arm isolates the compiler’s\nvalue: audio continues to arrive at the model’s native cadence, but\ntrigger-to-chunk latency collapses from a 470470 ms median to\n∼35{\\sim}35 s—the stream simply stops tracking the performer. The replayed\ngate-on arm also reproduces the live session’s gating profile (43.9%43.9\\%\nvs. 43.1%43.1\\% absorbed), validating the replay methodology itself.\n\nTable 7: Gating ablation over input-identical replays of the primary\nsession. “e2e” is trigger→\\tochunk-received, reconstructed by the replay\nclient (∼3{\\sim}3–99 ms tighter than the live browser measurement); the\nstatic arm’s e2e reflects chunks no longer being attributable to recent\ninput rather than pipeline delay.\n\non\noff\nstatic\n\nGate decisions\n2,8702{,}870\n2,9462{,}946\n2,9462{,}946\n\nfired\n1,6111{,}611\n2,9462{,}946\n1010\n\nabsorbed\n43.9%43.9\\%\n0%0\\%\n99.7%99.7\\%\n\nAPI pushes (min-1)\n\n173173\n202202\n1.11.1\n\nQueue drops / reconnects / stream ends\n0\n0\n0\n\nChunk cadence median (ms)\n2,0062{,}006\n1,9991{,}999\n2,0052{,}005\n\ne2e median (ms)\n470470\n457457\n34,82834{,}828\n\n7 Discussion\n\n7.1 Why Prompt Compilation Works\n\nInterposing a natural-language prompt between the estimated performance state and\nthe generator—rather than exposing the model to raw MIDI, learned latents, or a\nbespoke control API—buys four properties at once.\n\nIt is a model-agnostic surface: every current text-conditioned music\nmodel (Google DeepMind, 2025; Copet et al., 2023; Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) accepts free-form prose,\nso compiling state into prose rather than into one model’s private\nparameterization treats the generator as an interchangeable backend, and\nretargeting is a matter of re-tuning phrasing and the parameter table rather than\nre-architecting the pipeline. It requires no training: no paired\nsoloist/accompaniment corpus, no gradient step, no adapter—all the musical\nknowledge the system contributes (Table 4) is deterministic,\nhuman-authored prose, which lets a small team encode substantial expertise\nwithout data-collection or training infrastructure. It is inspectable and\neditable clause by clause: every accompaniment decision is traceable to a\nspecific branch of the compiler, a musician can read the exact string sent to the\nmodel and edit the phrasing to change the behaviour, and—as the stray\nscale: bayati clause in Listing 3\nshows—compiler defects surface as legible English rather than as a silent shift\nin a latent. And it is deterministic, which is what makes gating\nsound at all: because the compiler is a pure function of state, the system can\ncheaply detect whether a re-prompt would be musically meaningful before paying\nfor it.\n\nThe measurements add a fifth, quantitative property. The knowledge-based layer\ncosts under a millisecond end to end (Fig. 3) against a\ngenerator budget three decades larger. Symbolic control of a streaming\nfoundation model is therefore not a performance compromise: on this pipeline the\nexpert system is free, and the design question is entirely about what the prose\nshould say and how often it should change.\n\nIn this respect MazzikaAI stands in the lineage of knowledge-based systems for\nmusic such as CHORAL (Ebcioğlu, 1988)—expertise captured in an\nexplicit, human-readable representation and applied by deterministic rules—with\nthe effector replaced. That substitution is the transferable idea. The\narchitecture it embodies (estimate live human state →\\to compile to prose →\\to\nsteer a streaming generator →\\to render →\\to loop) is not specific to music: the\nsame loop could plausibly drive live visuals from pose estimation, steer a text\ngenerator from a running model of a document and its recent edits, or extend code\nfrom the evolving structure of a program. In each case the load-bearing\nengineering is identical to ours—a deterministic, domain-informed compiler plus\na policy for when to re-steer. We do not claim to have validated these transfers;\nwe note only that the pattern appears reusable.\n\n7.2 Cultural and Microtonal Fidelity\n\nPrompt-level grounding is a lightweight and inspectable path toward non-Western\nidioms (Shahriar and Tariq, 2021; Bozkurt et al., 2014): it needs no specialized model and no\nretraining, and the entire cultural encoding is prose that a maqam expert can\nread, critique, and correct. But a lightweight instructional path is not faithful\nrendering, and the distinction must be drawn sharply. The prompt can\ninstruct the model to shimmer on a half-flat E and to slide\nD→E​-half-flatD\\!\\rightarrow\\!E\\text{-half-flat}; whether the model renders that degree\nat its true pitch is a separate question governed by its training data. General\ntext-to-music models are trained predominantly on twelve-tone equal-tempered\nWestern material (Agostinelli et al., 2023; Copet et al., 2023), and their\ninternal representation of pitch may under-represent quarter-tone intervals\nentirely. The pitch analysis of §6.4 now gives this\nquestion a measured, partial answer. Grounding does not merely decorate: it\nanchors generation on the prescribed tonic and moves a significantly larger\nshare of the melodic line off the equal-tempered grid, with the second degree\nvisited predominantly in the half-flat band. But the aggregate tuning\nevidence is equally clear that the half-flat second is not rendered as a\nstable scale degree—the model inflects toward the quarter-tone\nrather than tuning to it. Whether that inflection-without-anchoring profile\nis heard by maqam-trained musicians as expressive authenticity or as\nout-of-tune approximation is a perceptual question that pitch measurement\nalone cannot settle; it is a central hypothesis for the controlled listening\nstudy planned as future work (§7.4).\n\n7.3 Limitations\n\nWe describe MazzikaAI as a working system and a methodological contribution, not\nan empirically validated result. Five limitations bound the claims.\n\nNo large-scale user study. We report instrumented system\nmeasurements, objective output-quality ablations, and a qualitative\nperceptual evaluation with two expert musicians (§6.3);\na controlled listening study with a larger participant pool remains to be\nrun. The live measurement session was performed by the first author, and the\nablation arms replay that same performance, so behavioural coverage across\nplayers and genres remains to be established.\n\nConstraint language is not honoured. Because the API exposes no\nstem-level control, unwanted instruments are addressed purely through negative\nprompt language—and the audit of §6.4 shows this\nmechanism failing: excluded-instrument detections were more frequent\nwith the silence clause than without it. More broadly the whole\ncontrol scheme assumes the generator obeys constraint-style instructions\n(avoid these tonalities, exclude vocals, hold this tempo), and when it does\nnot, the compiler has no lower-level fallback; per-stem API control, where it\nbecomes available, is the principled fix.\n\nDependence on a proprietary hosted model, and a latency floor set by it.\nLyria RealTime is closed and subject to availability, quota and behavioural\nchange outside our control; its inability to re-tempo a live stream shapes the\narchitecture, and its 22 s chunk granularity sets the floor beneath the\nmeasured 263263 ms median. Two service-side disconnects in our own log\n(§6.2) illustrate the exposure.\n\nNarrow, hand-authored coverage in places. Harmonic inference is rule-based\nand partly blues-specific—the implied-chord logic infers a twelve-bar A7/D7/E7\nregion from a pitch-class histogram and does not generalize to arbitrary keys\nwithout further rules. The state estimator models one player on one keyboard and\nhas no notion of multiple simultaneous performers. Voice commands are\nEnglish-only, a notable gap given the system’s Arabic-music focus. And gesture\ncontrol depends on lighting, camera placement and a hold-plus-cooldown protocol,\nand has not been evaluated under stage conditions.\n\n7.4 Future Work\n\nFour directions follow directly from the measurements. Beat tracking and\ntempo following is the priority: estimate the performer’s tempo and beat phase\nfrom the onset stream—the estimator already computes notes-per-second and\ninter-onset gaps—and re-anchor both engines to it, absorbing the reconnect cost\nat musically safe boundaries such as phrase ends and long idles.\nRecalibrating the gate is now a measurable exercise rather than a guess:\ncoarsen the harmony fingerprint or add hysteresis, and sweep re-prompt frequency\nagainst perceived coherence—the gating ablation establishes that stability is\nnot the binding constraint, so the sweep is bounded only by musical coherence.\nPerceptual validation means scaling the expert playing sessions of\n§6.3 into a controlled listening study, whose hypotheses\nthe executed ablations have sharpened;\nthe replay harness additionally enables objective MIR measures not yet\ncomputed, such as harmonic agreement with the soloist and rhythmic alignment.\nAnd reducing platform dependence means\nretargeting the compiler to an open-weights streaming\nmodel (Lyria Team, Google DeepMind, 2025) for on-device operation, which would remove both the\nnetwork latency floor and the proprietary-API exposure. Further out, a hybrid\ncompiler could tune phrasing or timing from performance data while retaining the\ninspectable text interface, the estimator could track several simultaneous\nsoloists, and per-stem control—if future APIs expose it—would replace\nbest-effort prompt suppression with guaranteed mixing.\n\n8 Conclusion\n\nWe presented MazzikaAI, a real-time accompaniment system built around one\nmethodological idea: a deterministic, musically-informed compiler from live\nperformance state to natural-language prompts. Architecturally it is a\nknowledge-based system in the classical sense—an inspectable hand-authored\nknowledge base, a working memory holding the estimated performance state, and a\nrule-based four-state inference layer—whose actuator is prose consumed by a\npretrained streaming generator rather than a symbolic effector. That substitution\nis what lets the system obtain idiomatic, microtonal call-and-response\naccompaniment for Arabic maqam performance with no fine-tuning of any\nkind—and to retarget to eight further Western genres at the cost of\nauthoring prompt fragments.\n\nInstrumenting the deployed pipeline sharpens the claim in both directions. The\nknowledge-based layer costs under a millisecond against a generator budget three\ndecades larger, so symbolic control of a streaming foundation model is\nessentially free; the median key-to-audible-update latency of 263263 ms lands at\nthe front of the answer window the controller itself opens; and the stream\ntolerates 179179 re-prompts per minute without a single failure—and, in the\ngating ablation, 202202 pushes per minute with the gate disabled entirely.\nControlled ablations over input-identical replayed sessions attribute\nbehaviour to design choices: maqam grounding shifts generation toward the\ntonic and yields significantly more quarter-tone melodic content; a static\nprompt lets trigger-to-audio latency collapse to tens of seconds, quantifying\nwhat the compiler buys. The same instrumentation also marks the current\nlimits of prompt-level control—most notably, constraint-style prohibition\nis no substitute for stem-level mixing. A perceptual evaluation with two\nexpert musicians localizes the remaining gap precisely: the compiler controls\nwhat the accompaniment plays far more accurately than when it\nplays it.\n\nThe broader significance is that much of the intelligence a responsive creative\npartner needs can be located not in model weights but in a transparent,\nknowledge-based compiler that translates live human state into language a general\nmodel already understands—and that on current hardware this layer is cheap\nenough to be free. As streaming generative models improve, we expect the value of\nsuch lightweight, inspectable, idiom-aware control layers to grow.\n\nCRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nJiaxin Du: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Investigation,\nWriting – original draft, Writing – review & editing.\nYong Zhuang: Validation, Investigation, Writing – review & editing.\nHaoyu Li: Investigation, Writing – review & editing.\n\nDeclaration of competing interest\n\nThe authors declare no known competing financial interests or personal\nrelationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this\npaper.\n\nData availability\n\nThe replication package for this article is deposited on figshare\n(DOI: 10.6084/m9.figshare.XXXXXXX). The deposit contains\nthe system source code; the primary-session instrumentation log underlying\nFigs. 3–5 and\nTables 5–6; the replay harness, condition\nflags, and per-arm session logs for every ablation arm of\n§6.4 (each log records its own condition in its first\nrecord); a run manifest mapping arms to logs; the analysis scripts and\ntheir JSON outputs behind Fig. 6 and\nTable 7; and the generated audio chunks\n(∼420{\\sim}420 MB) for every reported experiment arm. Replaying an arm\nreproduces the input exactly; because the deployed generative model (Google\nLyria RealTime) is a hosted stochastic service accessed through its public\nAPI, regenerated audio varies across runs, and the deposited audio and\nanalysis outputs document the arms reported here.\n\nAppendix A Genre Catalogue\n\nBeyond its Arabic-maqam core, the compiler carries eight further Western\ngenres (Table 8). They exist chiefly as evidence for the\nadaptation-cost claim of §2: each was added by authoring a\ngenre configuration—section list and order, per-role prompt language, a\nlead-preference list, header text, generation-parameter rows, and percussion\npatterns—with no change to the estimator, controller, or transport. Arabic\nand blues use bespoke controllers with idiom-specific harmonic and echo\nlogic; the remaining genres share a generic controller driven entirely by\nthe configuration record. Output quality outside the Arabic design centre is\nnot evaluated in this paper and is expected to be weaker, in proportion to\nthe depth of each genre’s authored knowledge.\n\nTable 8: The nine genres: instrument sections (low→\\rightarrowhigh), lead\ninstrument, and controller.\n\nGenre\nSections (low→\\rightarrowhigh)\nLead\nController\n\nvintage arabic\ncello, oud, strings, qanun, nay\nnay\nbespoke (takht)\n\nblues\nbass, rhythm gtr, piano, harmonica, lead gtr\nharmonica\nbespoke\n\njazz fusion\nupright bass, piano, jazz gtr, sax, trumpet\nsaxophone\ngeneric\n\nrock & roll\nbass, rhythm gtr, piano/keys, lead gtr\nlead guitar\ngeneric\n\nclassical\ncello, viola, violin, oboe\noboe\ngeneric\n\nmodern pop\nbass synth, pad synth, keys, vocal aah\nvocal aah\ngeneric\n\nflamenco\ncello, violin, flamenco gtr\nflamenco gtr\ngeneric\n\ncinematic\ncello, strings, brass pad, piano/harp\npiano\ngeneric\n\nfunk\nbass, rhythm gtr, keys/organ, horns\nhorn section\ngeneric\n\nReferences\n\nA. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. Frank (2023)\nMusicLM: generating music from text.\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2301.11325.\n\nCited by: §1,\n§2,\nTable 1,\n§7.2.\n\nZ. Borsos, R. Marinier, D. Vincent, E. Kharitonov, O. Pietquin, M. Sharifi, D. Roblek, O. Teboul, D. Grangier, M. Tagliasacchi, and N. Zeghidour (2023)\nAudioLM: a language modeling approach to audio generation.\n\nIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing 31, pp. 2523–2533.\n\nExternal Links: Document\n\nCited by: §2.\n\nB. Bozkurt, R. Ayangil, and A. Holzapfel (2014)\nComputational analysis of Turkish makam music: review of state-of-the-art and challenges.\n\nJournal of New Music Research 43 (1), pp. 3–23.\n\nExternal Links: Document\n\nCited by: §1,", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1600, "totalLines": 2437}}}
